window.GM_INDEX = [{"url":"cheatsheet.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Cheat Sheets","num":"cheatsheet","heads":["Foundations Module 1","Bases & bits","Ranges & magnitudes","Floating point","Linear algebra","C, Pointers & Memory Module 3","Pointers","Stack vs heap","Undefined behavior","Build & debug","Hardware & the Roofline Module 4","CPU vs GPU","Memory hierarchy","Roofline"],"summary":"Dense, printable reference for the whole course — the numbers, formulas, APIs, and patterns worth keeping at your elbow. Use your browser's Print (or Save as PDF) for a clean paper copy.","text":"cheat sheets cheat sheets foundations module 1 bases & bits ranges & magnitudes floating point linear algebra c, pointers & memory module 3 pointers stack vs heap undefined behavior build & debug hardware & the roofline module 4 cpu vs gpu memory hierarchy roofline host ↔ device cuda kernels module 5 launch & index qualifiers & memory performance levers patterns & tools ml / ai infrastructure track a core ideas precision & attention scale-out inference & compile hpc & scientific track b libraries first patterns multi-node clusters & rigor real-time graphics track c the pipeline shaders & textures lighting ray tracing & vulkan cross-vendor & portable track d the landscape warp-size trap portability layers honest benchmarking dense, printable reference for the whole course — the numbers, formulas, apis, and patterns worth keeping at your elbow. use your browser's print (or save as pdf) for a clean paper copy."},{"url":"concept-map.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Concept Map","num":"conceptmap","heads":[],"summary":"The whole curriculum as one dependency map: a core path that takes you from raw math to writing real CUDA, then four specialization tracks that branch from it. Click any stage to expand its lessons.","text":"concept map concept map  the whole curriculum as one dependency map: a core path that takes you from raw math to writing real cuda, then four specialization tracks that branch from it. click any stage to expand its lessons."},{"url":"curriculum.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Curriculum","num":"m0l1","heads":["Core · Module 0 — The Big Picture BUILT — start here","Core · Module 1 — Mathematical Foundations BUILT","Core · Module 2 — Programming from Zero (Python) BUILT","Core · Module 3 — C & C++ for GPU Work BUILT","Core · Module 4 — How Computers & GPUs Work BUILT","Core · Module 5 — CUDA Fundamentals BUILT — CORE COMPLETE","Track A — ML / AI Infrastructure Engineer BUILT — 11 lessons","Track B — HPC / Scientific Computing BUILT — 10 lessons","Track C — Graphics / Game Engines BUILT — 11 lessons","Track D — Vendor-Neutral / Portable GPU BUILT — 9 lessons"],"summary":"One shared core (49 lessons) takes you from school math to confident CUDA. Then four career tracks — treat them as four end-to-end courses sharing a foundation. Total: ~90 lessons, realistically 250–400 hours of honest work. Budget 6–12 months part-time; there is no shortcut, only a good map.","text":"curriculum the full curriculum core · module 0 — the big picture built — start here core · module 1 — mathematical foundations built core · module 2 — programming from zero (python) built core · module 3 — c & c++ for gpu work built core · module 4 — how computers & gpus work built core · module 5 — cuda fundamentals built — core complete track a — ml / ai infrastructure engineer built — 11 lessons track b — hpc / scientific computing built — 10 lessons track c — graphics / game engines built — 11 lessons track d — vendor-neutral / portable gpu built — 9 lessons one shared core (49 lessons) takes you from school math to confident cuda. then four career tracks — treat them as four end-to-end courses sharing a foundation. total: ~90 lessons, realistically 250–400 hours of honest work. budget 6–12 months part-time; there is no shortcut, only a good map."},{"url":"exam.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Job-Readiness Exam","num":"exam","heads":[],"summary":"A timed, scored exam drawn from every module and track — the same questions the course drills, shuffled fresh each attempt. Score 70% or higher to pass, then review every answer with the explanation and a link back to the lesson.","text":"job-readiness exam job-readiness exam  a timed, scored exam drawn from every module and track — the same questions the course drills, shuffled fresh each attempt. score 70% or higher to pass, then review every answer with the explanation and a link back to the lesson."},{"url":"glossary.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Glossary","num":"glossary","heads":["'+esc(e.t)+ (e.a&&e.a.length?' ('+esc(e.a.join(', '))+') ':'')+'"],"summary":"Every core term, defined in plain English and linked to the lesson that teaches it. On any lesson, hover a dashed term for its definition inline.","text":"glossary glossary '+esc(e.t)+ (e.a&&e.a.length?' ('+esc(e.a.join(', '))+') ':'')+' every core term, defined in plain english and linked to the lesson that teaches it. on any lesson, hover a dashed term for its definition inline."},{"url":"index.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"GPU Mastery — from school math to shipped GPU code","num":"home","heads":["One core, four careers","Mathematical foundations → CUDA fundamentals","ML / AI Infrastructure","HPC / Scientific Computing","Graphics / Game Engines","Vendor-Neutral / Portable GPU","Built to a single standard","The 80%, then the depth","Measured, not asserted","Job-ready, in your words","Reason with the fundamentals, don't just read them →","Study it & get hired","Review Deck","Interview Prep"],"summary":"A single 49-lesson core takes you from mathematics to confident CUDA. Then choose a track — each an end-to-end course sharing that foundation. Pick where you want to end up.","text":"gpu mastery — from school math to shipped gpu code master the gpu, from the first principle to the shipped frame. one core, four careers mathematical foundations → cuda fundamentals ml / ai infrastructure hpc / scientific computing graphics / game engines vendor-neutral / portable gpu built to a single standard the 80%, then the depth measured, not asserted job-ready, in your words reason with the fundamentals, don't just read them → study it & get hired review deck interview prep job-readiness exam concept map how to use it a single 49-lesson core takes you from mathematics to confident cuda. then choose a track — each an end-to-end course sharing that foundation. pick where you want to end up."},{"url":"interview.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Interview Prep","num":"interview","heads":[],"summary":"Fifty-plus real GPU and CUDA interview questions, grouped by topic and difficulty, each with a model answer and a link to the lesson that teaches it. Reveal answers one at a time, or filter to the area you're drilling and reveal them all.","text":"interview prep interview prep  fifty-plus real gpu and cuda interview questions, grouped by topic and difficulty, each with a model answer and a link to the lesson that teaches it. reveal answers one at a time, or filter to the area you're drilling and reveal them all."},{"url":"lab-amdahl.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"Amdahl's Law Explorer","num":"lab-amdahl","heads":["How to read it"],"summary":"The most sobering — and most useful — limit in all of parallel computing (Lesson 0.4). Speed up the parallel part of a program as much as you like; the serial part you can't parallelize sets a hard ceiling on the whole thing. Dial the two knobs below and watch the wall appear.","text":"amdahl's law explorer amdahl's law explorer how to read it the most sobering — and most useful — limit in all of parallel computing (lesson 0.4). speed up the parallel part of a program as much as you like; the serial part you can't parallelize sets a hard ceiling on the whole thing. dial the two knobs below and watch the wall appear."},{"url":"lab-coalescing.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"Coalescing / Sector Visualizer","num":"lab-coalescing","heads":["What it teaches"],"summary":"The highest-leverage GPU skill: reading an index expression and seeing the memory traffic it orders (5.7). Pick an access pattern and watch a 32-lane warp's addresses land in 32-byte sectors — and how many bytes get fetched but wasted.","text":"coalescing / sector visualizer coalescing / sector visualizer what it teaches the highest-leverage gpu skill: reading an index expression and seeing the memory traffic it orders (5.7). pick an access pattern and watch a 32-lane warp's addresses land in 32-byte sectors — and how many bytes get fetched but wasted."},{"url":"lab-floatbits.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"Float-Format Bit Explorer","num":"lab-floatbits","heads":["What it teaches"],"summary":"A number is meaningless until you know how it's stored (M1 L8). Type a value, pick a format from fp32 down to fp4, and see exactly which bits hold it, what value actually gets stored, and how much you lost — the precision-vs-range trade behind mixed precision (A6) made visible.","text":"float-format bit explorer float-format bit explorer what it teaches a number is meaningless until you know how it's stored (m1 l8). type a value, pick a format from fp32 down to fp4, and see exactly which bits hold it, what value actually gets stored, and how much you lost — the precision-vs-range trade behind mixed precision (a6) made visible."},{"url":"lab-matmul.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"WebGPU Matmul Lab","num":"lab-matmul","heads":["How to read it","The kernels (WGSL)"],"summary":"Two kernels compute the exact same matrix product on your GPU: naive (every thread re-reads its row and column straight from global memory) and tiled (every thread first stages a block into fast on-chip shared memory and reuses it). Same math, same answer — the only difference is how many times the GPU has to go to mem","text":"webgpu matmul lab webgpu matmul lab — the tiling win, live on your gpu how to read it the kernels (wgsl) two kernels compute the exact same matrix product on your gpu: naive (every thread re-reads its row and column straight from global memory) and tiled (every thread first stages a block into fast on-chip shared memory and reuses it). same math, same answer — the only difference is how many times the gpu has to go to memory. that gap is lesson 5.5's whole point, made visible instead of asserted."},{"url":"lab-montecarlo.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"Monte Carlo Convergence Lab","num":"lab-montecarlo","heads":["How to read it"],"summary":"Every Monte Carlo method — pricing an option, simulating neutron transport, integrating a function nobody can solve in closed form — rests on one law: throw enough random samples at a problem and the average converges to the truth, with error shrinking as 1/ N . This lab makes that law visible instead of asserted: watc","text":"monte carlo convergence lab monte carlo convergence lab how to read it every monte carlo method — pricing an option, simulating neutron transport, integrating a function nobody can solve in closed form — rests on one law: throw enough random samples at a problem and the average converges to the truth, with error shrinking as 1/ n . this lab makes that law visible instead of asserted: watch emerge from random points, and watch the error track its predicted curve exactly."},{"url":"lab-occupancy.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"Occupancy Calculator","num":"lab-occupancy","heads":["What it teaches"],"summary":"Occupancy is how many warps are resident per SM versus the max — the dial from 5.6. It's set by three budgets competing for the SM: registers, shared memory, and thread/block slots. Turn each knob and watch which resource binds and what occupancy you actually get.","text":"occupancy calculator occupancy calculator what it teaches occupancy is how many warps are resident per sm versus the max — the dial from 5.6. it's set by three budgets competing for the sm: registers, shared memory, and thread/block slots. turn each knob and watch which resource binds and what occupancy you actually get."},{"url":"lab-pbr.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"PBR Material Explorer","num":"lab-pbr","heads":["How to read it"],"summary":"Every physically based renderer — game engine, offline movie renderer, this course's Track C capstone — shades a surface with the same three-term Cook-Torrance BRDF : a distribution term, a geometry term, and a Fresnel term. This lab computes that exact math, per pixel, live in your browser (no WebGL, just canvas pixel","text":"pbr material explorer pbr material explorer how to read it every physically based renderer — game engine, offline movie renderer, this course's track c capstone — shades a surface with the same three-term cook-torrance brdf : a distribution term, a geometry term, and a fresnel term. this lab computes that exact math, per pixel, live in your browser (no webgl, just canvas pixels), so you can watch what each parameter actually does instead of taking the formula on faith."},{"url":"lab-roofline.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"Roofline Explorer","num":"lab-roofline","heads":["How to read it"],"summary":"The single most important performance mental model (4.7), made live. Set a machine's compute and bandwidth ceilings, dial a kernel's arithmetic intensity, and watch where it lands — memory-bound or compute-bound, and how far below its ceiling. \"The gap is your to-do list.\"","text":"roofline explorer roofline explorer how to read it the single most important performance mental model (4.7), made live. set a machine's compute and bandwidth ceilings, dial a kernel's arithmetic intensity, and watch where it lands — memory-bound or compute-bound, and how far below its ceiling. \"the gap is your to-do list.\""},{"url":"labs.html","kind":"lab","group":"Interactive Lab","title":"Interactive Labs","num":"labs","heads":["Reason with it — calculators & visualizers","Amdahl's Law Explorer →","Roofline Explorer →","Occupancy Calculator →","Coalescing Visualizer →","Float-Format Bit Explorer →","Run it — hands-on GPU labs","WebGPU Reduction Lab →","WebGPU Matmul Lab →","The Stride Cliff, measured","Go deeper on your track — visualizers","Monte Carlo Convergence Lab →","PBR Material Explorer →","Remember it & get hired — study tools"],"summary":"Knowledge you only read decays. The way to make the fundamentals yours is to poke at them until the mental models are muscle memory. These labs come in four flavors: calculators & visualizers for the core mental models, hands-on labs that run real GPU code in your browser, track visualizers that make a specialization's","text":"interactive labs interactive labs reason with it — calculators & visualizers amdahl's law explorer → roofline explorer → occupancy calculator → coalescing visualizer → float-format bit explorer → run it — hands-on gpu labs webgpu reduction lab → webgpu matmul lab → the stride cliff, measured go deeper on your track — visualizers monte carlo convergence lab → pbr material explorer → remember it & get hired — study tools review deck → interview prep → job-readiness exam → glossary → cheat sheets → concept map → knowledge you only read decays. the way to make the fundamentals yours is to poke at them until the mental models are muscle memory. these labs come in four flavors: calculators & visualizers for the core mental models, hands-on labs that run real gpu code in your browser, track visualizers that make a specialization's core idea live instead of asserted, and a full study loop — spaced repetition, interview prep, and reference — that keeps it all in your head."},{"url":"prompt.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Generator Prompt","num":"mXlY","heads":["Why this prompt is shaped this way"],"summary":"This prompt built Module 1 and will build every other module of this curriculum to the same standard. Paste it into a capable LLM (Claude, etc.), set the MODULE line, and attach or reference an existing lesson file so the model can mirror the exact HTML conventions.","text":"generator prompt the module generator prompt why this prompt is shaped this way this prompt built module 1 and will build every other module of this curriculum to the same standard. paste it into a capable llm (claude, etc.), set the module line, and attach or reference an existing lesson file so the model can mirror the exact html conventions."},{"url":"review.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Review — Spaced Repetition","num":"review","heads":[],"summary":"Spaced repetition schedules each card so it comes back just before you'd forget it — the fastest known way to move facts into long-term memory. Cards are auto-built from every glossary term and quiz in the course. Your progress is saved in this browser only.","text":"review — spaced repetition review  spaced repetition schedules each card so it comes back just before you'd forget it — the fastest known way to move facts into long-term memory. cards are auto-built from every glossary term and quiz in the course. your progress is saved in this browser only."},{"url":"search.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Search","num":"search","heads":[],"summary":"Every lesson, lab, and hub — search by concept (coalescing, roofline, PBR, FlashAttention, wavefront…). Press / anywhere on the site to search from any page.","text":"search search the course  every lesson, lab, and hub — search by concept (coalescing, roofline, pbr, flashattention, wavefront…). press / anywhere on the site to search from any page."},{"url":"setup.html","kind":"hub","group":"Hub","title":"Lab Setup","num":"","heads":["Path 1 — Google Colab (recommended start: free, zero install)","Path 2 — your own NVIDIA GPU (any GTX 10-series or newer works)","Windows: use WSL2 (the professional setup)","Linux: native","Path 3 — no NVIDIA GPU, other hardware","Editor & tools (all free)","Using this site as your desktop app"],"summary":"Straight talk: no app can bundle a GPU. Real CUDA practice needs either NVIDIA hardware you own or a free cloud GPU. Both paths below are zero-cost to start; pick one now, add the other later. Module 1 needs no setup at all — start learning today, set this up before Module 2.","text":"lab setup lab setup — your practice environment path 1 — google colab (recommended start: free, zero install) path 2 — your own nvidia gpu (any gtx 10-series or newer works) windows: use wsl2 (the professional setup) linux: native path 3 — no nvidia gpu, other hardware editor & tools (all free) using this site as your desktop app straight talk: no app can bundle a gpu. real cuda practice needs either nvidia hardware you own or a free cloud gpu. both paths below are zero-cost to start; pick one now, add the other later. module 1 needs no setup at all — start learning today, set this up before module 2."},{"url":"module-0/lesson-01.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"0.1 What a Computer Actually Does","num":"Module 0 · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","A program is a list of absurdly simple steps","The fetch–execute loop — the heartbeat of every processor","The clock — what \"3 GHz\" actually measures","Memory: a giant wall of numbered boxes","Why this matters for GPUs","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. What an instruction really is","2. The loop, pipelined","3. Why the clock is only part of the story","4. The memory wall — the theme beneath everything"],"summary":"A GPU is a special kind of computer, so before we can see what makes it special, we need a clear picture of what any computer does. It all comes from one humble idea: a processor reads a list of absurdly simple instructions and carries them out, one after another, billions of times a second. Get this picture right and ","text":"0.1 what a computer actually does what a computer actually does — instructions, clocks & the fetch–execute loop concept a program is a list of absurdly simple steps the fetch–execute loop — the heartbeat of every processor the clock — what \"3 ghz\" actually measures memory: a giant wall of numbered boxes why this matters for gpus worked example key points quiz exercises 1. what an instruction really is 2. the loop, pipelined 3. why the clock is only part of the story 4. the memory wall — the theme beneath everything 5. sequential by default — the road that leads to the gpu gotchas scenario — the mystery of the idle supercomputer practice a gpu is a special kind of computer, so before we can see what makes it special, we need a clear picture of what any computer does. it all comes from one humble idea: a processor reads a list of absurdly simple instructions and carries them out, one after another, billions of times a second. get this picture right and every later idea — cores, threads, memory bandwidth, the whole reason gpus exist — has somewhere to attach."},{"url":"module-0/lesson-02.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"0.2 The CPU — Built for Latency","num":"Module 0 · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","The CPU's one goal: low latency","Caches: keep the data close so you rarely wait","Branch prediction: guess so the pipeline never stalls","Out-of-order & superscalar: do more than one thing per tick","The shape this produces: a few big, clever cores","Why this is the wrong tool for a million identical jobs","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The memory hierarchy, with numbers","2. Locality, and the cache line","3. Speculation: fast, but not free, and not without risk"],"summary":"Last lesson we found the villain: waiting on memory. The CPU is a machine built around one obsession — finish the task in front of it as fast as humanly (electronically) possible, which means never waiting if it can help it. Understanding how it fights the wait, and what that costs, is the perfect mirror for understand","text":"0.2 the cpu — built for latency the cpu — built for latency concept the cpu's one goal: low latency caches: keep the data close so you rarely wait branch prediction: guess so the pipeline never stalls out-of-order & superscalar: do more than one thing per tick the shape this produces: a few big, clever cores why this is the wrong tool for a million identical jobs worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the memory hierarchy, with numbers 2. locality, and the cache line 3. speculation: fast, but not free, and not without risk 4. the ceiling on instruction-level parallelism 5. why the cpu can't just \"add thousands of cores\" gotchas scenario — the loop that got 10× faster without changing the math practice last lesson we found the villain: waiting on memory. the cpu is a machine built around one obsession — finish the task in front of it as fast as humanly (electronically) possible, which means never waiting if it can help it. understanding how it fights the wait, and what that costs, is the perfect mirror for understanding the gpu, which makes the opposite bet."},{"url":"module-0/lesson-03.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"0.3 The GPU — Built for Throughput","num":"Module 0 · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","A different goal: throughput, not latency","Spend the transistors on arithmetic","SIMT — one instruction, many threads","Latency hiding — the master trick","The bargain you must uphold","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. SIMT, precisely — and the shared program counter","2. Warp divergence — the cost of an if","3. Coalescing — why 32 neighbors should read 32 neighbors","4. Occupancy — enough warps to hide the wait"],"summary":"The CPU spends its silicon making a few streams fast and never waiting. The GPU makes the opposite bet: fill the chip with thousands of simple cores, accept that any one of them is slow and stalls constantly, and win by keeping so much work in flight that the whole machine is never idle. It turns the memory-wait proble","text":"0.3 the gpu — built for throughput the gpu — built for throughput concept a different goal: throughput, not latency spend the transistors on arithmetic simt — one instruction, many threads latency hiding — the master trick the bargain you must uphold worked example key points quiz exercises 1. simt, precisely — and the shared program counter 2. warp divergence — the cost of an if 3. coalescing — why 32 neighbors should read 32 neighbors 4. occupancy — enough warps to hide the wait 5. the gpu's memory: built for bandwidth 6. where the gpu loses gotchas scenario — the pixel shader and the price of one if practice the cpu spends its silicon making a few streams fast and never waiting. the gpu makes the opposite bet: fill the chip with thousands of simple cores, accept that any one of them is slow and stalls constantly, and win by keeping so much work in flight that the whole machine is never idle. it turns the memory-wait problem — the cpu's enemy — into its superpower."},{"url":"module-0/lesson-04.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"0.4 CPU vs GPU — The Trade-off","num":"Module 0 · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","The difference, in one table","The decision rule","They're a team: host and device","Amdahl's law — the serial part is your ceiling","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The copy tax, quantified","2. Amdahl vs Gustafson — why huge GPUs still make sense","3. Heterogeneous computing — use both, deliberately","4. Beyond speed: throughput per watt and per dollar","5. A checklist you can actually use"],"summary":"You now know two machines with opposite personalities: the CPU minimizes latency, the GPU maximizes throughput. This lesson turns that contrast into something you can act on — a rule for which tool a problem wants, how the two work as a team, and the honest ceiling (Amdahl's law) that stops even a perfect GPU from maki","text":"0.4 cpu vs gpu — the trade-off cpu vs gpu — the trade-off concept the difference, in one table the decision rule they're a team: host and device amdahl's law — the serial part is your ceiling worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the copy tax, quantified 2. amdahl vs gustafson — why huge gpus still make sense 3. heterogeneous computing — use both, deliberately 4. beyond speed: throughput per watt and per dollar 5. a checklist you can actually use gotchas scenario — the port that made everything slower practice you now know two machines with opposite personalities: the cpu minimizes latency, the gpu maximizes throughput. this lesson turns that contrast into something you can act on — a rule for which tool a problem wants, how the two work as a team, and the honest ceiling (amdahl's law) that stops even a perfect gpu from making a whole program infinitely fast."},{"url":"module-0/lesson-05.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"0.5 How the GPU Evolved","num":"Module 0 · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","Born to draw triangles (1999)","Programmable shaders crack the box open (early 2000s)","The GPGPU hack: computing in disguise (mid-2000s)","CUDA (2006): the GPU becomes a real computer","The deep learning explosion (2012 onward)","Today: purpose-built AI accelerators","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. From fixed-function to programmable","2. Why CUDA became a moat","3. The AI feedback loop"],"summary":"The chip that trains today's largest AI models was invented to make video games look good. Understanding how a graphics accelerator became the engine of the AI revolution isn't just history — it explains why GPUs are shaped the way they are, why one company's software ended up dominating, and why the skills in this cou","text":"0.5 how the gpu evolved how the gpu evolved — from drawing triangles to running the ai world concept born to draw triangles (1999) programmable shaders crack the box open (early 2000s) the gpgpu hack: computing in disguise (mid-2000s) cuda (2006): the gpu becomes a real computer the deep learning explosion (2012 onward) today: purpose-built ai accelerators worked example key points quiz exercises 1. from fixed-function to programmable 2. why cuda became a moat 3. the ai feedback loop 4. precision: less is more (for ai) 5. the scale, in numbers 6. where it's heading — and why it doesn't change your job gotchas scenario — the hack that became an industry practice the chip that trains today's largest ai models was invented to make video games look good. understanding how a graphics accelerator became the engine of the ai revolution isn't just history — it explains why gpus are shaped the way they are, why one company's software ended up dominating, and why the skills in this course sit exactly where the value is."},{"url":"module-0/lesson-06.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"0.6 The GPU Landscape & Your Roadmap","num":"Module 0 · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","Who makes GPUs, and what you program them with","Where GPUs run the world","Your roadmap through this course","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Consumer vs data-center vs cloud — and how to get access","2. The vendor competition, honestly","3. Beyond GPUs — the accelerator zoo","4. Where GPUs run the world, in detail","5. The roadmap, in full","6. How to stay valuable as the hardware races ahead"],"summary":"You now understand what a GPU is and why it exists. This closing lesson zooms out to the real world: who makes GPUs, what you actually program them with, where they run the modern economy, and — most importantly — exactly how the rest of this course carries you from these first ideas to a professional skill you can bui","text":"0.6 the gpu landscape & your roadmap the gpu landscape & your roadmap concept who makes gpus, and what you program them with where gpus run the world your roadmap through this course worked example key points quiz exercises 1. consumer vs data-center vs cloud — and how to get access 2. the vendor competition, honestly 3. beyond gpus — the accelerator zoo 4. where gpus run the world, in detail 5. the roadmap, in full 6. how to stay valuable as the hardware races ahead gotchas scenario — the engineer who bet on fundamentals practice you now understand what a gpu is and why it exists. this closing lesson zooms out to the real world: who makes gpus, what you actually program them with, where they run the modern economy, and — most importantly — exactly how the rest of this course carries you from these first ideas to a professional skill you can build a career on."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-01.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.1 Numbers, Bases & Binary","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","Counting in tens is a choice, not a law","Reading binary: place value with powers of 2","Hexadecimal: binary for humans","Negative numbers: two's complement","Fixed width and overflow","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Place value, generalized","2. The conversion toolkit","3. Two's complement, exactly","4. Overflow across your future stack"],"summary":"Every index, address, mask, and pixel a GPU touches is a binary number. This lesson rebuilds counting from scratch so that binary, hex, and overflow become obvious instead of mysterious.","text":"1.1 numbers, bases & binary numbers, bases & binary — how machines count concept counting in tens is a choice, not a law reading binary: place value with powers of 2 hexadecimal: binary for humans negative numbers: two's complement fixed width and overflow worked example key points quiz exercises 1. place value, generalized 2. the conversion toolkit 3. two's complement, exactly 4. overflow across your future stack 5. bitwise operations — the tools this unlocks 6. sizes, addresses, and what a pointer really is 7. gotchas scenario — the 3-billion-element bug hunt practice every index, address, mask, and pixel a gpu touches is a binary number. this lesson rebuilds counting from scratch so that binary, hex, and overflow become obvious instead of mysterious."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-02.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.2 Algebra as Computation","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","Variables: named boxes, with one big twist","Expressions and precedence","Functions: machines with one job","Solving = inverting: you still need it","Σ and Π: the loop you can already read","Inequalities: the language of bounds checking","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The full precedence story (the part that bites)","2. Functions, composed and curried by hand","3. Inverting formulas: a reliable algorithm"],"summary":"School algebra and programming are the same activity wearing different clothes. This lesson translates between them — and lands on the two formulas (index linearization and ceiling division) that appear in literally every GPU kernel.","text":"1.2 algebra as computation algebra as computation — variables, functions & the σ you'll write daily concept variables: named boxes, with one big twist expressions and precedence functions: machines with one job solving = inverting: you still need it σ and π: the loop you can already read inequalities: the language of bounds checking worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the full precedence story (the part that bites) 2. functions, composed and curried by hand 3. inverting formulas: a reliable algorithm 4. summation identities worth owning 5. launch-geometry algebra (the cuda sizing kit) 6. gotchas scenario — the resize that corrupted the dataset practice school algebra and programming are the same activity wearing different clothes. this lesson translates between them — and lands on the two formulas (index linearization and ceiling division) that appear in literally every gpu kernel."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-03.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.3 Exponents, Logs & Orders of Magnitude","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","Exponents from zero (literally)","Scientific notation and the two tribes of powers","Logarithms: exponents, asked backwards","Growth rates: the table that prices algorithms","FLOP/s and the estimation habit","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The complete exponent laws (and the classic misreads)","2. The log toolbox, ending at every softmax on Earth","3. Big-O without the folklore","4. Estimation discipline: the three-number method"],"summary":"GPU work is a numbers game played across 18 orders of magnitude — nanoseconds to seconds, one FLOP to 10¹⁵ per second. This lesson gives you exponent fluency, log intuition, and the estimation habit that lets pros predict a kernel's runtime before writing it.","text":"1.3 exponents, logs & orders of magnitude exponents, logarithms & orders of magnitude — thinking in powers concept exponents from zero (literally) scientific notation and the two tribes of powers logarithms: exponents, asked backwards growth rates: the table that prices algorithms flop/s and the estimation habit worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the complete exponent laws (and the classic misreads) 2. the log toolbox, ending at every softmax on earth 3. big-o without the folklore 4. estimation discipline: the three-number method 5. powers in the wild: growth and decay you'll meet 6. gotchas scenario — the fusion decision, made by arithmetic practice gpu work is a numbers game played across 18 orders of magnitude — nanoseconds to seconds, one flop to 10¹⁵ per second. this lesson gives you exponent fluency, log intuition, and the estimation habit that lets pros predict a kernel's runtime before writing it."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-04.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.4 Geometry, Coordinates & Trigonometry","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","Coordinates: naming every point in space","Distance: Pythagoras, promoted to n dimensions","Angles: degrees for humans, radians for machines","sin & cos: coordinates on the unit circle","Rotation: trig's day job in graphics","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Pythagoras: the one-minute proof","2. The unit circle as master key","3. Radians, definitively","4. atan2: the inverse that actually works"],"summary":"Every pixel has coordinates; every camera, rotation, wave, and circle in graphics — and every distance between embeddings in ML — reduces to Pythagoras and the unit circle. This lesson rebuilds both until they're reflexes.","text":"1.4 geometry, coordinates & trigonometry geometry, coordinates & trigonometry — the math of space concept coordinates: naming every point in space distance: pythagoras, promoted to n dimensions angles: degrees for humans, radians for machines sin & cos: coordinates on the unit circle rotation: trig's day job in graphics worked example key points quiz exercises 1. pythagoras: the one-minute proof 2. the unit circle as master key 3. radians, definitively 4. atan2: the inverse that actually works 5. sin as a wave: amplitude, frequency, phase 6. aspect ratio and normalized coordinates 7. gotchas scenario — the minimap that lied practice every pixel has coordinates; every camera, rotation, wave, and circle in graphics — and every distance between embeddings in ml — reduces to pythagoras and the unit circle. this lesson rebuilds both until they're reflexes."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-05.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.5 Vectors & the Dot Product","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","A vector is two things at once","The two operations that respect the arrow","Length and normalization","The dot product: one number measuring alignment","Cross product: the perpendicular-maker (3D only)","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Why Σ aᵢbᵢ equals ‖a‖‖b‖cos θ","2. Projection: the shadow, formalized","3. Building frames: from one vector to a full basis","4. Vectors in hardware: float4, SoA, and the SIMT preview"],"summary":"Strip away the frameworks and a GPU spends its life doing one thing: dot products, billions per millisecond. Lighting a pixel, firing a neuron, ranking a document — same operation. This lesson makes vectors and their one killer product completely yours.","text":"1.5 vectors & the dot product vectors & the dot product — the atom of gpu work concept a vector is two things at once the two operations that respect the arrow length and normalization the dot product: one number measuring alignment cross product: the perpendicular-maker (3d only) worked example key points quiz exercises 1. why σ aᵢbᵢ equals ‖a‖‖b‖cos θ 2. projection: the shadow, formalized 3. building frames: from one vector to a full basis 4. vectors in hardware: float4, soa, and the simt preview 5. dot products at industrial scale (the bridge to everything) 6. gotchas scenario — the retrieval system that preferred long documents practice strip away the frameworks and a gpu spends its life doing one thing: dot products, billions per millisecond. lighting a pixel, firing a neuron, ranking a document — same operation. this lesson makes vectors and their one killer product completely yours."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-06.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.6 Matrices & Matrix Multiplication","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","A matrix is a function you can store","Matrix × matrix: composing two functions","The cost: 2mnk, and why GPUs exist","Storage: the row-major / column-major schism","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The rule is forced, not chosen","2. The transform zoo (2D; 3D is the same idea per-axis)","3. Homogeneous coordinates: how translation sneaks in","4. Chain economics, formalized","5. Transpose: backprop's favorite operator"],"summary":"A matrix is a grid of numbers that acts : it moves every point in space at once. Matrix multiplication is the single most-executed heavy computation on Earth — every neural network layer, every 3D camera, every physics solve. Learn it slowly here; you'll optimize it for the rest of the course.","text":"1.6 matrices & matrix multiplication matrices — the operation gpus were built for concept a matrix is a function you can store matrix × matrix: composing two functions the cost: 2mnk, and why gpus exist storage: the row-major / column-major schism worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the rule is forced, not chosen 2. the transform zoo (2d; 3d is the same idea per-axis) 3. homogeneous coordinates: how translation sneaks in 4. chain economics, formalized 5. transpose: backprop's favorite operator 6. strides: how numpy/pytorch really store matrices 7. gotchas scenario — the 40 ms frame that was one flag away from 12 ms practice a matrix is a grid of numbers that acts : it moves every point in space at once. matrix multiplication is the single most-executed heavy computation on earth — every neural network layer, every 3d camera, every physics solve. learn it slowly here; you'll optimize it for the rest of the course."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-07.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.7 Probability & Statistics Essentials","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","Probability: the fraction of worlds","Random variables, expectation, variance","Distributions: uniform and normal cover 90% of your needs","Statistics: telling the truth about noisy measurements","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Pseudo-randomness: deterministic chaos with a seed","2. Randomness across 100,000 threads","3. The Central Limit Theorem, operationally","4. Initialization: the variance calculation that unlocked deep learning","5. Expectation as a design tool: dropout and stochastic estimates"],"summary":"GPUs meet randomness twice: as a tool (Monte Carlo simulation, dropout, weight init, path tracing) and as a fact of life (your kernel's timings jitter; your benchmark lies without statistics). This lesson covers exactly the probability a GPU professional uses weekly.","text":"1.7 probability & statistics essentials probability & statistics — randomness as a tool, noise as a fact concept probability: the fraction of worlds random variables, expectation, variance distributions: uniform and normal cover 90% of your needs statistics: telling the truth about noisy measurements worked example key points quiz exercises 1. pseudo-randomness: deterministic chaos with a seed 2. randomness across 100,000 threads 3. the central limit theorem, operationally 4. initialization: the variance calculation that unlocked deep learning 5. expectation as a design tool: dropout and stochastic estimates 6. tails, percentiles, and the slo mindset 7. gotchas scenario — the \"flaky\" benchmark that was three real bugs practice gpus meet randomness twice: as a tool (monte carlo simulation, dropout, weight init, path tracing) and as a fact of life (your kernel's timings jitter; your benchmark lies without statistics). this lesson covers exactly the probability a gpu professional uses weekly."},{"url":"module-1/lesson-08.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"1.8 Floating Point","num":"Module 1 · Lesson 8","heads":["Concept","The problem: infinite reals, 32 bits","Relative precision: dense near zero, sparse far out","Why 0.1 + 0.2 ≠ 0.3","Special values: the algebra of the edges","The format zoo of 2026","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Rounding: the default and the alternatives","2. Absorption and catastrophic cancellation","3. Summation: order is an algorithm","4. FMA: one rounding instead of two"],"summary":"Module 1's capstone, and the single most bug-generating topic in numerical computing. fp16 overflow NaN'd real training runs; reduction order makes GPU results \"randomly\" differ from CPU; 0.1 + 0.2 ≠ 0.3. After this lesson, none of that will ever surprise you again.","text":"1.8 floating point floating point — how gpus really store numbers concept the problem: infinite reals, 32 bits relative precision: dense near zero, sparse far out why 0.1 + 0.2 ≠ 0.3 special values: the algebra of the edges the format zoo of 2026 worked example key points quiz exercises 1. rounding: the default and the alternatives 2. absorption and catastrophic cancellation 3. summation: order is an algorithm 4. fma: one rounding instead of two 5. gpu-specific float behavior 6. comparing floats like a professional 7. gotchas scenario — the model that failed accuracy review by 0.4% practice module 1's capstone, and the single most bug-generating topic in numerical computing. fp16 overflow nan'd real training runs; reduction order makes gpu results \"randomly\" differ from cpu; 0.1 + 0.2 ≠ 0.3. after this lesson, none of that will ever surprise you again."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-01.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.1 Your First Program","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","What a program actually is","Variables: it's an action, not an equation","The types you'll use 95% of the time","Strings and f-strings: making output speak","Converting between types (and when Python refuses)","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. What really happens when you press Run","2. The object model: three facts that predict everything","3. Numbers, completely","4. Strings, enough to be dangerous"],"summary":"Today you stop reading code and start writing it. You already own the math (Module 1); Python is just the fastest way to make a machine do that math. By the end of this lesson your first program will be a genuinely professional artifact: a GPU kernel-time estimator.","text":"2.1 your first program your first program — values, variables & types concept what a program actually is variables: it's an action, not an equation the types you'll use 95% of the time strings and f-strings: making output speak converting between types (and when python refuses) worked example key points quiz exercises 1. what really happens when you press run 2. the object model: three facts that predict everything 3. numbers, completely 4. strings, enough to be dangerous 5. input, validation, and your first defensive pattern 6. gotchas scenario — the estimator that made the meeting practice today you stop reading code and start writing it. you already own the math (module 1); python is just the fastest way to make a machine do that math. by the end of this lesson your first program will be a genuinely professional artifact: a gpu kernel-time estimator."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-02.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.2 Control Flow","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","Booleans: conditions are values","if / elif / else — and indentation IS the syntax","while: repeat until the world changes","for + range: counted repetition, half-open by design","Nested loops: grids, and who spins fastest","The four loop patterns that are 90% of all loops","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Loop tools that mark you as fluent","2. The loop-else: obscure, occasionally perfect","3. Never loop on floats"],"summary":"Straight-line programs compute one thing. Add if (choose) and loops (repeat) and you can compute anything — that's not rhetoric, it's a theorem. Today you also run your first simulated GPU launch: 12 threads, a ragged last block, and the guard that saves it.","text":"2.2 control flow control flow — decisions and repetition concept booleans: conditions are values if / elif / else — and indentation is the syntax while: repeat until the world changes for + range: counted repetition, half-open by design nested loops: grids, and who spins fastest the four loop patterns that are 90% of all loops worked example key points quiz exercises 1. loop tools that mark you as fluent 2. the loop-else: obscure, occasionally perfect 3. never loop on floats 4. nested-loop cost discipline 5. truthiness, precisely 6. match: the modern multi-way branch 7. gotchas scenario — the binary converter that met its edge cases practice straight-line programs compute one thing. add if (choose) and loops (repeat) and you can compute anything — that's not rhetoric, it's a theorem. today you also run your first simulated gpu launch: 12 threads, a ragged last block, and the guard that saves it."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-03.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.3 Functions & Scope","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","def: from formula to machine","The call stack: where parameters live","Scope: how Python finds a name","Defaults, keywords, and calls that read like sentences","Purity: the property GPUs will pay you for","Worked example","Key points","Try it live","Quiz","Exercises","1. Functions are objects — pass them around","2. *args and **kwargs: variadic machines","3. Closures: functions that remember their birthplace"],"summary":"M1 L2 called functions \"machines with one job.\" Today you build the machines: define them, pass data in, get results out, and understand exactly where names live. Plus the design property — purity — that will decide, in Module 5, whether your code can run on 10,000 threads.","text":"2.3 functions & scope functions & scope — building your own machines concept def: from formula to machine the call stack: where parameters live scope: how python finds a name defaults, keywords, and calls that read like sentences purity: the property gpus will pay you for worked example key points try it live quiz exercises 1. functions are objects — pass them around 2. *args and **kwargs: variadic machines 3. closures: functions that remember their birthplace 4. recursion: elegant, priced, and bounded 5. type hints: documentation that tools can check 6. function design rules (the code-review checklist) 7. gotchas scenario — the accumulator that couldn't be tested practice m1 l2 called functions \"machines with one job.\" today you build the machines: define them, pass data in, get results out, and understand exactly where names live. plus the design property — purity — that will decide, in module 5, whether your code can run on 10,000 threads."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-04.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.4 Lists, Tuples, Dicts & Sets","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","Lists: the workhorse sequence","Aliasing: the tag picture gets dangerous","Tuples: immutable sequences, perfect returns","Dicts: names for data, O(1)-ish lookup","Sets: membership and dedup, nothing else, blazingly","Comprehensions: build collections as expressions","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Slices, completely","2. How dicts really work (interview-grade version)","3. Choosing a container: the decision table"],"summary":"One variable per value stops scaling at about five values. Collections are how programs hold thousands — and how Python's \"names are tags\" picture finally shows its teeth. Today you also multiply your first real matrices and verify them against Module 1's hand computation.","text":"2.4 lists, tuples, dicts & sets lists, tuples, dicts & sets — organizing many values concept lists: the workhorse sequence aliasing: the tag picture gets dangerous tuples: immutable sequences, perfect returns dicts: names for data, o(1)-ish lookup sets: membership and dedup, nothing else, blazingly comprehensions: build collections as expressions worked example key points quiz exercises 1. slices, completely 2. how dicts really work (interview-grade version) 3. choosing a container: the decision table 4. what a list is underneath (and why append is cheap) 5. nested structures: the shapes of real data 6. == vs is, hashing, and the equality dossier 7. gotchas scenario — the corrupted occupancy table practice one variable per value stops scaling at about five values. collections are how programs hold thousands — and how python's \"names are tags\" picture finally shows its teeth. today you also multiply your first real matrices and verify them against module 1's hand computation."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-05.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.5 NumPy & Vectorized Thinking","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","Why: the 100× that's been waiting","dtype: Module 1's number formats become a parameter","Shape, axes, and whole-array expressions","Broadcasting: the rule that writes your loops","Views vs copies: the sharp edge (you're pre-trained)","Matmul: the oracle meets the machine","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The ndarray, formally (four fields explain everything)","2. Broadcasting, edge cases included","3. Views vs copies: the definitive table"],"summary":"This is the lesson where Module 1 and Module 2 fuse. NumPy packs numbers the way hardware wants them (M1 L1/L8: fixed-width, contiguous) and runs whole-array operations in compiled code (M1 L5: one op, many lanes). Learn to think in arrays here and CUDA will feel like a dialect, not a new language.","text":"2.5 numpy & vectorized thinking numpy & vectorized thinking — your first taste of throughput concept why: the 100× that's been waiting dtype: module 1's number formats become a parameter shape, axes, and whole-array expressions broadcasting: the rule that writes your loops views vs copies: the sharp edge (you're pre-trained) matmul: the oracle meets the machine worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the ndarray, formally (four fields explain everything) 2. broadcasting, edge cases included 3. views vs copies: the definitive table 4. temporaries, out=, and the fusion preview 5. random numbers, the modern way 6. float rules, imported wholesale 7. gotchas scenario — the etl job that fit in ram after all practice this is the lesson where module 1 and module 2 fuse. numpy packs numbers the way hardware wants them (m1 l1/l8: fixed-width, contiguous) and runs whole-array operations in compiled code (m1 l5: one op, many lanes). learn to think in arrays here and cuda will feel like a dialect, not a new language."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-06.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.6 Errors, Debugging & Testing","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","Tracebacks: read the autopsy bottom-up","Raising and handling: exceptions are control flow for failure","Debugging: the scientific method, not the shotgun","Assertions: executable assumptions","pytest: tests as tiny facts","Testing floats: tolerances are engineering, not settings","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The hierarchy, and making your own","2. Fixtures: shared setup without shared state","3. Property-based thinking (test laws, not examples)"],"summary":"Code that runs is a rumor; code that's tested is a claim. This lesson makes you fluent in Python's error machinery, systematic (not superstitious) at debugging, and armed with pytest — including the float-tolerance discipline that separates numerical engineers from numerical gamblers.","text":"2.6 errors, debugging & testing errors, debugging & testing — engineering trust concept tracebacks: read the autopsy bottom-up raising and handling: exceptions are control flow for failure debugging: the scientific method, not the shotgun assertions: executable assumptions pytest: tests as tiny facts testing floats: tolerances are engineering, not settings worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the hierarchy, and making your own 2. fixtures: shared setup without shared state 3. property-based thinking (test laws, not examples) 4. logging: print that scales 5. the numerical-testing playbook (used for every kernel from here on) 6. gotchas scenario — the test that failed every fiftieth tuesday practice code that runs is a rumor; code that's tested is a claim. this lesson makes you fluent in python's error machinery, systematic (not superstitious) at debugging, and armed with pytest — including the float-tolerance discipline that separates numerical engineers from numerical gamblers."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-07.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.7 Files, Modules & Environments","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","Files are bytes; everything else is interpretation","Structured files: CSV and JSON in ten lines","Paths: pathlib, not string surgery","Modules: import runs your file (once)","Environments: the deployability ritual","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. with, generalized: acquisition has a shape","2. Import mechanics for debugging","3. Package layout: the two conventions that matter","4. Configs: from constants to files"],"summary":"\"Readily deployable\" starts here. Data must survive the process ending (files), code must be reusable across projects (modules), and your setup must be reproducible on machines you've never touched (environments). This is the lesson that turns a folder of experiments into a repo a stranger can run.","text":"2.7 files, modules & environments files, modules & environments — from scripts to software concept files are bytes; everything else is interpretation structured files: csv and json in ten lines paths: pathlib, not string surgery modules: import runs your file (once) environments: the deployability ritual worked example key points quiz exercises 1. with, generalized: acquisition has a shape 2. import mechanics for debugging 3. package layout: the two conventions that matter 4. configs: from constants to files 5. environment troubleshooting: the four-question protocol 6. gotchas scenario — \"works on my machine,\" dissected in four acts practice \"readily deployable\" starts here. data must survive the process ending (files), code must be reusable across projects (modules), and your setup must be reproducible on machines you've never touched (environments). this is the lesson that turns a folder of experiments into a repo a stranger can run."},{"url":"module-2/lesson-08.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"2.8 Capstone: Image Processing with NumPy","num":"Module 2 · Lesson 8","heads":["The project brief","Stage 0 — the data model (read this twice)","Stage 1 — grayscale: a dot product per pixel (M1 L5)","Stage 2 — brightness & contrast: the uint8 minefield (M1 L1/L8)","Stage 3 — box blur: neighborhood means via shifted views (M1 L7 + 2.5)","Stage 4 — edges: finite differences (Track A1/B1's opening act)","Stage 5 — vignette: broadcasting a radial mask (M1 L4 + 2.5)","Stage 6 — downscale: the reshape-pool trick (2.5's finale)","Worked example — assembling the pipeline","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Timing the pipeline honestly (M1 L7, mechanized)","2. Sepia and per-pixel matrix multiply (M1 L6 cameo)"],"summary":"Everything converges: pixels are arrays (M1's promise), filters are the math you own, and the deliverable is a tested, deployable repo. You'll build an image pipeline — grayscale, brightness, blur, edges, vignette, downscale — where every operation is a Module 1 lesson wearing work clothes. This project, on GPU, is lit","text":"2.8 capstone: image processing with numpy capstone — image processing with numpy the project brief stage 0 — the data model (read this twice) stage 1 — grayscale: a dot product per pixel (m1 l5) stage 2 — brightness & contrast: the uint8 minefield (m1 l1/l8) stage 3 — box blur: neighborhood means via shifted views (m1 l7 + 2.5) stage 4 — edges: finite differences (track a1/b1's opening act) stage 5 — vignette: broadcasting a radial mask (m1 l4 + 2.5) stage 6 — downscale: the reshape-pool trick (2.5's finale) worked example — assembling the pipeline key points quiz exercises 1. timing the pipeline honestly (m1 l7, mechanized) 2. sepia and per-pixel matrix multiply (m1 l6 cameo) 3. views, copies, and the pipeline's memory story 4. gotchas scenario — shipping pixelforge practice — extensions (each one is a mini-project) everything converges: pixels are arrays (m1's promise), filters are the math you own, and the deliverable is a tested, deployable repo. you'll build an image pipeline — grayscale, brightness, blur, edges, vignette, downscale — where every operation is a module 1 lesson wearing work clothes. this project, on gpu, is literally module 5's curriculum."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-01.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.1 Why C — Source to Binary","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","Why a Python person learns C (the honest pitch)","hello.c, honestly annotated","The pipeline: four machines in a trench coat","Static typing: errors move left","printf: formatted output with a loaded gun","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Optimization levels: what the dial does","2. The preprocessor: powerful, dumb, everywhere","3. Assembly literacy: the 20-minute version","4. Why C is fast: the memory model IS the answer"],"summary":"CUDA is a C++ dialect; drivers, runtimes, and every kernel you'll ever ship live on this side of the fence. Module 2 made you productive; Module 3 makes you close to the machine . First step: watch your text become a program, stage by stage — because when builds break (and they will), this pipeline is the map.","text":"3.1 why c — source to binary why c — and what really happens between source and binary concept why a python person learns c (the honest pitch) hello.c, honestly annotated the pipeline: four machines in a trench coat static typing: errors move left printf: formatted output with a loaded gun worked example key points quiz exercises 1. optimization levels: what the dial does 2. the preprocessor: powerful, dumb, everywhere 3. assembly literacy: the 20-minute version 4. why c is fast: the memory model is the answer 5. the professional default (copy this into your makefile) 6. gotchas scenario — the build that broke three ways before lunch practice cuda is a c++ dialect; drivers, runtimes, and every kernel you'll ever ship live on this side of the fence. module 2 made you productive; module 3 makes you close to the machine . first step: watch your text become a program, stage by stage — because when builds break (and they will), this pipeline is the map."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-02.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.2 Types, Sizes & Undefined Behavior","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","The type zoo — and the stdint discipline","Implicit conversions: the compiler's quiet hand","Overflow: two different universes","Floats in C: Module 1 Lesson 8, verbatim","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The conversion ladder, precisely","2. The UB catalog (the ones you will actually meet)","3. Undefined vs implementation-defined vs unspecified","4. Metadata headers: asking the platform about itself","5. One expression, three rulebooks: C / NumPy / CUDA"],"summary":"Module 1 taught you fixed-width arithmetic as math; NumPy re-introduced it as dtypes. C is where it becomes law — with a twist Python never had: break certain rules and the compiler is allowed to assume you didn't , then optimize accordingly. This lesson makes you fluent in the rules and fluent in the consequences.","text":"3.2 types, sizes & undefined behavior types, sizes & undefined behavior — the rules of the metal concept the type zoo — and the stdint discipline implicit conversions: the compiler's quiet hand overflow: two different universes floats in c: module 1 lesson 8, verbatim worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the conversion ladder, precisely 2. the ub catalog (the ones you will actually meet) 3. undefined vs implementation-defined vs unspecified 4. metadata headers: asking the platform about itself 5. one expression, three rulebooks: c / numpy / cuda 6. gotchas scenario — the checksum that failed only on the arm build server practice module 1 taught you fixed-width arithmetic as math; numpy re-introduced it as dtypes. c is where it becomes law — with a twist python never had: break certain rules and the compiler is allowed to assume you didn't , then optimize accordingly. this lesson makes you fluent in the rules and fluent in the consequences."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-03.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.3 Pointers","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","The two operators: & takes an address, * follows one","Pass-by-value, and why swap needs pointers","Out-parameters: C's multi-return","Pointer-to-pointer: the cudaMalloc pattern, demystified forever","const and pointers: who may write what","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Reading declarations without tears","2. void*: the generic address","3. Function pointers: behavior as a value (M2 L3, compiled)","4. Casting and strict aliasing — the honest version"],"summary":"Here it is: the concept every CUDA API, every kernel signature, and every C interview orbits. The good news — you've been preparing for three modules: M1 L1 gave you addresses as numbers, M2 L1/L4 gave you names-point-at-objects. A pointer is just that arrow, handed to you as a value .","text":"3.3 pointers pointers — addresses as values concept the two operators: & takes an address, * follows one pass-by-value, and why swap needs pointers out-parameters: c's multi-return pointer-to-pointer: the cudamalloc pattern, demystified forever const and pointers: who may write what worked example key points quiz exercises 1. reading declarations without tears 2. void*: the generic address 3. function pointers: behavior as a value (m2 l3, compiled) 4. casting and strict aliasing — the honest version 5. alignment: addresses have shapes 6. gotchas scenario — the api you could suddenly read practice here it is: the concept every cuda api, every kernel signature, and every c interview orbits. the good news — you've been preparing for three modules: m1 l1 gave you addresses as numbers, m2 l1/l4 gave you names-point-at-objects. a pointer is just that arrow, handed to you as a value ."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-04.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.4 Arrays, Strings & Pointer Arithmetic","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","Arrays: contiguous, fixed, fast","Pointer arithmetic: indexing IS address math","Decay: functions receive an address, not an array","2D data: one buffer + the linearization you've owned since M1","Strings: byte arrays with a promise","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The mem* family: bulk bytes, no types","2. The string toolbox, safety-annotated","3. AoS vs SoA in C — the trilogy completes","4. Multidimensional layouts, formally"],"summary":"A C array is M2 L5's ndarray with the safety equipment removed: contiguous bytes, a pointer, and you as the bounds checker. Master the arithmetic here — every CUDA kernel you ever write is this lesson executed by 10,000 threads at once.","text":"3.4 arrays, strings & pointer arithmetic arrays, strings & pointer arithmetic — contiguous memory, unguarded concept arrays: contiguous, fixed, fast pointer arithmetic: indexing is address math decay: functions receive an address, not an array 2d data: one buffer + the linearization you've owned since m1 strings: byte arrays with a promise worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the mem* family: bulk bytes, no types 2. the string toolbox, safety-annotated 3. aos vs soa in c — the trilogy completes 4. multidimensional layouts, formally 5. iteration idioms and the one-past-end law 6. gotchas scenario — the benchmark table that ate its own header practice a c array is m2 l5's ndarray with the safety equipment removed: contiguous bytes, a pointer, and you as the bounds checker. master the arithmetic here — every cuda kernel you ever write is this lesson executed by 10,000 threads at once."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-05.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.5 The Stack, the Heap & malloc","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","The memory map: four neighborhoods, four lifetimes","malloc / free: the contract","realloc and growth: Python's list, hand-built","The GPU mirror (why this lesson is non-negotiable)","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Inside malloc: why allocation isn't free (pun intended)","2. Error-path hygiene: the one sanctioned goto","3. Stack economics: when locals are wrong","4. Ownership patterns that scale beyond one function","5. Alignment & calloc's quiet superpowers"],"summary":"Python freed memory when the last tag fell off (M2 L1). C hands you the whole lifecycle: request bytes, use them, return them — exactly once, at the right time, on every code path. This discipline IS cudaMalloc/cudaFree's discipline; learn it where the tools (3.8) can catch you.","text":"3.5 the stack, the heap & malloc the stack, the heap & malloc — you are the garbage collector now concept the memory map: four neighborhoods, four lifetimes malloc / free: the contract realloc and growth: python's list, hand-built the gpu mirror (why this lesson is non-negotiable) worked example key points quiz exercises 1. inside malloc: why allocation isn't free (pun intended) 2. error-path hygiene: the one sanctioned goto 3. stack economics: when locals are wrong 4. ownership patterns that scale beyond one function 5. alignment & calloc's quiet superpowers 6. gotchas scenario — the overnight run that ate the workstation practice python freed memory when the last tag fell off (m2 l1). c hands you the whole lifecycle: request bytes, use them, return them — exactly once, at the right time, on every code path. this discipline is cudamalloc/cudafree's discipline; learn it where the tools (3.8) can catch you."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-06.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.6 Structs, Headers & the Build","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","struct: a record with an address layout","Padding: the layout you didn't ask for","Headers: promises shared across compilation units","make: the dependency graph that ends stale binaries","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Struct semantics: value type, all the way","2. Binary I/O: structs on disk, and why it's a trap you must know","3. Header hygiene at scale (the rules that prevent spaghetti)","4. make, industrial grade (still 20 lines)","5. CMake: the 8 lines that carry you to CUDA"],"summary":"One file was fine for learning; real code is many files sharing types and promises. Today: structs (records with a memory layout you control), headers (the promises), and make (the machine that rebuilds exactly what changed). This is M2 L7's repo ritual, compiled — and it's the skeleton CMake will wrap around your CUDA","text":"3.6 structs, headers & the build structs, headers & the build — from files to projects concept struct: a record with an address layout padding: the layout you didn't ask for headers: promises shared across compilation units make: the dependency graph that ends stale binaries worked example key points quiz exercises 1. struct semantics: value type, all the way 2. binary i/o: structs on disk, and why it's a trap you must know 3. header hygiene at scale (the rules that prevent spaghetti) 4. make, industrial grade (still 20 lines) 5. cmake: the 8 lines that carry you to cuda 6. gotchas scenario — the 40-minute build that became 40 seconds practice one file was fine for learning; real code is many files sharing types and promises. today: structs (records with a memory layout you control), headers (the promises), and make (the machine that rebuilds exactly what changed). this is m2 l7's repo ritual, compiled — and it's the skeleton cmake will wrap around your cuda projects."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-07.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.7 C++ Essentials for CUDA","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","References: the pointer that can't be wrong","RAII: the destructor is a free() that cannot be forgotten","std::vector: FloatBuf, retired with honors","Value semantics: the opposite default from Python","Templates-lite: dtype as a compile-time parameter","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The interop seam: name mangling and extern \"C\"","2. std::string: char buffers, retired for host code","3. Range-for, auto, and the deduction rules that matter","4. Why templates live in headers (the two-phase truth)"],"summary":"CUDA is C++, but kernel code is C-with-templates, not enterprise OOP. This lesson teaches the precise subset you'll use: references, RAII, vector/string, and light templates — each one retiring a manual C discipline you just earned. Earning it first was the point: you'll know what the automation does.","text":"3.7 c++ essentials for cuda c++ essentials — exactly the subset cuda needs concept references: the pointer that can't be wrong raii: the destructor is a free() that cannot be forgotten std::vector: floatbuf, retired with honors value semantics: the opposite default from python templates-lite: dtype as a compile-time parameter worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the interop seam: name mangling and extern \"c\" 2. std::string: char buffers, retired for host code 3. range-for, auto, and the deduction rules that matter 4. why templates live in headers (the two-phase truth) 5. new failure modes c++ adds (know the diagnostics) 6. gotchas scenario — the wrapper that ended the leak class practice cuda is c++, but kernel code is c-with-templates, not enterprise oop. this lesson teaches the precise subset you'll use: references, raii, vector/string, and light templates — each one retiring a manual c discipline you just earned. earning it first was the point: you'll know what the automation does."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-08.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.8 Segfaults, gdb & Sanitizers","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 8","heads":["Concept","What a segfault actually is","The toolbox, matched to symptoms","gdb in ten commands (the traceback machine, interactive)","ASan: the crash you deserve, at the moment you earn it","The method is still M2 L6's","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Debugging optimized code (because -O0 isn't always available)","2. How ASan works — and therefore where it's blind","3. valgrind vs sanitizers, honestly","4. Defense-in-depth: making the debugger unnecessary"],"summary":"Python guarded every index and raised readable exceptions (M2 L6). C took the guards away — this lesson gives you better ones: a debugger that replays the crime, and sanitizers that catch memory bugs the instant they happen. Same scientific method as M2 L6; sharper instruments.","text":"3.8 segfaults, gdb & sanitizers segfaults, gdb & sanitizers — making c's silence loud concept what a segfault actually is the toolbox, matched to symptoms gdb in ten commands (the traceback machine, interactive) asan: the crash you deserve, at the moment you earn it the method is still m2 l6's worked example key points quiz exercises 1. debugging optimized code (because -o0 isn't always available) 2. how asan works — and therefore where it's blind 3. valgrind vs sanitizers, honestly 4. defense-in-depth: making the debugger unnecessary 5. the gpu bridge (why this lesson happened now) 6. gotchas scenario — the 1-in-200 crash that survived three \"fixes\" practice python guarded every index and raised readable exceptions (m2 l6). c took the guards away — this lesson gives you better ones: a debugger that replays the crime, and sanitizers that catch memory bugs the instant they happen. same scientific method as m2 l6; sharper instruments."},{"url":"module-3/lesson-09.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"3.9 Capstone: CPU Matmul & the Honest Ladder","num":"Module 3 · Lesson 9","heads":["The project brief","Stage 0 — repo + fixtures (the bridge from Module 2)","Stage 1 — naive, correct, measured","Stage 2 — the harness (M1 L7, compiled once and for all)","Stage 3 — the ladder (each rung is a lesson cashing out)","Worked example — the report that makes it real","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. restrict, -march=native, and the last legal hints","2. Reading perf's verdict (the profiler preview)","3. What OpenBLAS knows that you now almost do","4. Gotchas","Scenario — the interview that was this module"],"summary":"Everything in this module converges: build matmul in C, prove it correct against your own oracle chain (hand → Python → NumPy → C), then climb the performance ladder — loop order, blocking, all cores — measuring honestly at every rung. The number you end with is the baseline the GPU must beat in 5.11, and the method yo","text":"3.9 capstone: cpu matmul & the honest ladder capstone — cpu matmul & the honest ladder the project brief stage 0 — repo + fixtures (the bridge from module 2) stage 1 — naive, correct, measured stage 2 — the harness (m1 l7, compiled once and for all) stage 3 — the ladder (each rung is a lesson cashing out) worked example — the report that makes it real key points quiz exercises 1. restrict, -march=native, and the last legal hints 2. reading perf's verdict (the profiler preview) 3. what openblas knows that you now almost do 4. gotchas scenario — the interview that was this module practice — extensions everything in this module converges: build matmul in c, prove it correct against your own oracle chain (hand → python → numpy → c), then climb the performance ladder — loop order, blocking, all cores — measuring honestly at every rung. the number you end with is the baseline the gpu must beat in 5.11, and the method you use is the method for the rest of your career."},{"url":"module-4/lesson-01.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"4.1 Inside a CPU Core","num":"Module 4 · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","The contract: fetch, decode, execute — forever","Trick 1 — pipelining: an assembly line for instructions","Trick 2 — branch prediction: guessing to keep the line moving","Trick 3 — superscalar + out-of-order: width, if you feed it","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Reading the core's own diary: perf","2. GHz is a negotiation, not a number","3. Hyperthreading: two warps per CPU core (almost)","4. The cmov economy (when branchless wins, precisely)","5. The translation table (start of the module-long bridge)"],"summary":"Module 3 ended with measured mysteries; this module explains them from silicon up. First: what a core actually does with your instructions — and why \"3 GHz\" tells you almost nothing about speed. Every idea here reappears, mutated, inside a GPU SM in 4.4.","text":"4.1 inside a cpu core inside a cpu core — pipelines, predictions & the meaning of ipc 0.4 concept the contract: fetch, decode, execute — forever trick 1 — pipelining: an assembly line for instructions trick 2 — branch prediction: guessing to keep the line moving trick 3 — superscalar + out-of-order: width, if you feed it worked example key points quiz exercises 1. reading the core's own diary: perf 2. ghz is a negotiation, not a number 3. hyperthreading: two warps per cpu core (almost) 4. the cmov economy (when branchless wins, precisely) 5. the translation table (start of the module-long bridge) 6. gotchas scenario — the \"faster server\" that lost the bake-off practice module 3 ended with measured mysteries; this module explains them from silicon up. first: what a core actually does with your instructions — and why \"3 ghz\" tells you almost nothing about speed. every idea here reappears, mutated, inside a gpu sm in 4.4."},{"url":"module-4/lesson-02.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"4.2 The Memory Hierarchy","num":"Module 4 · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","The hierarchy: a staircase of bets","The cache line: memory's minimum order size","Prefetching: the hardware reads ahead","Write policies and the dirty secret of stores","Multicore's tax: coherence and false sharing","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Associativity: why caches evict things that \"should\" fit","2. The TLB: the cache above the caches","3. Latency vs bandwidth: the ledger that predicts your ceiling","4. Streaming stores and the write ledger"],"summary":"M1 L3's ladder said one memory trip costs a thousand multiplies. This lesson shows the machinery that makes it survivable — caches, lines, prefetchers — and finishes explaining 3.9: the T=64 knee, the alignment shrug, and why blocked matmul's IPC jumped 5×. The GPU inherits every idea and rearranges the furniture (4.5)","text":"4.2 the memory hierarchy the memory hierarchy — where your cycles actually went concept the hierarchy: a staircase of bets the cache line: memory's minimum order size prefetching: the hardware reads ahead write policies and the dirty secret of stores multicore's tax: coherence and false sharing worked example key points quiz exercises 1. associativity: why caches evict things that \"should\" fit 2. the tlb: the cache above the caches 3. latency vs bandwidth: the ledger that predicts your ceiling 4. streaming stores and the write ledger 5. what the gpu keeps and what it discards (bridge to 4.5) 6. gotchas scenario — the resize that was 40× too slow, byte by byte practice m1 l3's ladder said one memory trip costs a thousand multiplies. this lesson shows the machinery that makes it survivable — caches, lines, prefetchers — and finishes explaining 3.9: the t=64 knee, the alignment shrug, and why blocked matmul's ipc jumped 5×. the gpu inherits every idea and rearranges the furniture (4.5)."},{"url":"module-4/lesson-03.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"4.3 The Parallelism Taxonomy","num":"Module 4 · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","Latency vs throughput: two products, not two qualities","The three shapes of parallel (you've used all three)","Amdahl's law: the serial fraction is a ceiling, not a tax","The two companions Amdahl travels with","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Task vs data parallelism (and why GPUs chose data)","2. Work and span: the law for trees and pipelines","3. Superlinear speedups: real, explainable, non-magical","4. The real currency: joules per result","5. CPU or GPU? The four-question gate"],"summary":"Before the GPU tour (next lesson), the vocabulary that makes it legible: the two processor philosophies, the three parallelism shapes you've already used, and the two laws — Amdahl's and the bandwidth roof — that dismembered 3.9's \"why only 5.6× on 8 threads?\" mystery.","text":"4.3 the parallelism taxonomy the parallelism taxonomy — latency, throughput & amdahl's bill concept latency vs throughput: two products, not two qualities the three shapes of parallel (you've used all three) amdahl's law: the serial fraction is a ceiling, not a tax the two companions amdahl travels with worked example key points quiz exercises 1. task vs data parallelism (and why gpus chose data) 2. work and span: the law for trees and pipelines 3. superlinear speedups: real, explainable, non-magical 4. the real currency: joules per result 5. cpu or gpu? the four-question gate 6. gotchas scenario — the \"gpu-ready\" pipeline that wasn't (and then was) practice before the gpu tour (next lesson), the vocabulary that makes it legible: the two processor philosophies, the three parallelism shapes you've already used, and the two laws — amdahl's and the bandwidth roof — that dismembered 3.9's \"why only 5.6× on 8 threads?\" mystery."},{"url":"module-4/lesson-04.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"4.4 Inside the GPU — SMs, Warps & Latency Hiding","num":"Module 4 · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","The floor plan: many modest cores, not one heroic one","The warp: 32 threads, one program counter (mostly)","Residency: warps LIVE on the SM","Latency hiding: the trick itself","Blocks and the grid: the software mirror","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Schedulers and dual-issue fine print","2. Independent thread scheduling: the modern truth about lockstep","3. Tensor cores: the SIMD-of-tiles fourth layer","4. What a block actually buys (and what it costs)"],"summary":"Here is the machine itself. One idea organizes all of it: a GPU hides memory latency not with caches and predictions, but with an enormous standing army of threads, any of which can issue the moment another stalls. Hold that sentence; every spec, limit, and CUDA rule in your future is a footnote to it.","text":"4.4 inside the gpu — sms, warps & latency hiding inside the gpu — sms, warps & the one trick that explains everything concept the floor plan: many modest cores, not one heroic one the warp: 32 threads, one program counter (mostly) residency: warps live on the sm latency hiding: the trick itself blocks and the grid: the software mirror worked example key points quiz exercises 1. schedulers and dual-issue fine print 2. independent thread scheduling: the modern truth about lockstep 3. tensor cores: the simd-of-tiles fourth layer 4. what a block actually buys (and what it costs) 5. tail effects and the wave arithmetic 6. gotchas scenario — the kernel review you can already conduct practice here is the machine itself. one idea organizes all of it: a gpu hides memory latency not with caches and predictions, but with an enormous standing army of threads, any of which can issue the moment another stalls. hold that sentence; every spec, limit, and cuda rule in your future is a footnote to it."},{"url":"module-4/lesson-05.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"4.5 The GPU Memory System","num":"Module 4 · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","The board: six levels, bandwidth-first","Coalescing: the warp-sized cache line, quantified","Shared memory banks: 32 tellers, one queue each","Atomics: the histogram problem, GPU edition","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The L2: the level nobody programs and everybody uses","2. Constant memory and the read-only path","3. The occupancy-vs-shared exchange rate (the real 5.5 negotiation)","4. Unified memory: the honest preview","5. The translation table, completed"],"summary":"Same staircase as 4.2, three renovations: the fast level is yours to manage (shared memory), the line is judged per-warp (coalescing), and the whole thing is sized for ten thousand tenants. Every Module 5 optimization is a move on this board.","text":"4.5 the gpu memory system the gpu memory system — the hierarchy you drive by hand concept the board: six levels, bandwidth-first coalescing: the warp-sized cache line, quantified shared memory banks: 32 tellers, one queue each atomics: the histogram problem, gpu edition worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the l2: the level nobody programs and everybody uses 2. constant memory and the read-only path 3. the occupancy-vs-shared exchange rate (the real 5.5 negotiation) 4. unified memory: the honest preview 5. the translation table, completed 6. gotchas scenario — the embedding-table serving fire practice same staircase as 4.2, three renovations: the fast level is yours to manage (shared memory), the line is judged per-warp (coalescing), and the whole thing is sized for ten thousand tenants. every module 5 optimization is a move on this board."},{"url":"module-4/lesson-06.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"4.6 The Host–Device Bridge","num":"Module 4 · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","The topology: read the widths","Transfer economics: the three regimes","Latency: the bridge has a toll booth, not just a width","Pinned memory and overlap: making the bridge work in parallel","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. NVLink: the ferry between islands","2. The GPUDirect family: deleting the middleman","3. Unified memory ON the bridge (closing 4.5 §4's loop)","4. Batch size: a bridge story wearing an ML costume","5. Gotchas"],"summary":"The fastest GPU code is worthless if the data spends its life on a 64 GB/s bridge between two multi-terabyte islands. This lesson prices the bridge, teaches the three tolls (latency, pinning, direction), and installs the discipline that shapes every production GPU system: data goes up once, stays, and only answers come","text":"4.6 the host–device bridge the host–device bridge — transfer economics & the art of not transferring concept the topology: read the widths transfer economics: the three regimes latency: the bridge has a toll booth, not just a width pinned memory and overlap: making the bridge work in parallel worked example key points quiz exercises 1. nvlink: the ferry between islands 2. the gpudirect family: deleting the middleman 3. unified memory on the bridge (closing 4.5 §4's loop) 4. batch size: a bridge story wearing an ml costume 5. gotchas scenario — the migration that paid for itself in an afternoon practice the fastest gpu code is worthless if the data spends its life on a 64 gb/s bridge between two multi-terabyte islands. this lesson prices the bridge, teaches the three tolls (latency, pinning, direction), and installs the discipline that shapes every production gpu system: data goes up once, stays, and only answers come down."},{"url":"module-4/lesson-07.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"4.7 Spec Sheets & the Roofline","num":"Module 4 · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","Reading a spec sheet without being marketed to","The roofline: the machine model as one picture","MFU: the honest scoreboard","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The roofline's fine print (what the simple chart hides)","2. The module's laws, on one card","3. Gotchas (the module's greatest hits, condensed)","Scenario — the module, cashed in one meeting","Practice"],"summary":"The module's graduation exercise: turn any spec sheet into a machine model, and any kernel into a dot on one chart that tells you — before profiling, before writing code — what its ceiling is and which lever moves it. This is the estimation habit from M1 L3, grown into a professional instrument.","text":"4.7 spec sheets & the roofline spec sheets & the roofline — one chart to locate every kernel concept reading a spec sheet without being marketed to the roofline: the machine model as one picture mfu: the honest scoreboard worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the roofline's fine print (what the simple chart hides) 2. the module's laws, on one card 3. gotchas (the module's greatest hits, condensed) scenario — the module, cashed in one meeting practice the module's graduation exercise: turn any spec sheet into a machine model, and any kernel into a dot on one chart that tells you — before profiling, before writing code — what its ceiling is and which lever moves it. this is the estimation habit from m1 l3, grown into a professional instrument."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-01.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.1 Lab Setup & First Compile","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","Your GPU options, honestly ranked","The verify ritual (60 seconds, every machine, forever)","nvcc: the 3.1 pipeline, twice","hello.cu — annotated","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Colab survival kit","2. Local: the flags file (3.6's Makefile grows CUDA rows)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the workshop that started with 40 broken laptops","Practice"],"summary":"Four modules of preparation end here: a real GPU runs your code today. This lesson gets the toolchain working (Colab or local), teaches nvcc as 3.1's pipeline-run-twice, and establishes the verify ritual you'll run on every machine you ever touch.","text":"5.1 lab setup & first compile lab setup & first compile — hardware, at last concept your gpu options, honestly ranked the verify ritual (60 seconds, every machine, forever) nvcc: the 3.1 pipeline, twice hello.cu — annotated worked example key points quiz exercises 1. colab survival kit 2. local: the flags file (3.6's makefile grows cuda rows) 3. gotchas scenario — the workshop that started with 40 broken laptops practice four modules of preparation end here: a real gpu runs your code today. this lesson gets the toolchain working (colab or local), teaches nvcc as 3.1's pipeline-run-twice, and establishes the verify ritual you'll run on every machine you ever touch."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-02.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.2 Your First Real Kernel","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","The inversion: from loop to identity","Launch geometry: ceil-divide, then guard","2D grids: images get coordinates","Grid-stride loops: one launch shape for any n","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. What >> actually passes (and how scalars travel)","2. Index dialects you'll read in the wild","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the review you can now give","Practice"],"summary":"saxpy on a million floats, for real. The kernel body you wrote in 3.4, the launch arithmetic from M1 L2, the guard from M2 L2, the hardware from 4.4 — today they compile together. One new skill: thinking \"my index\" instead of \"the loop.\"","text":"5.2 your first real kernel your first real kernel — threads, blocks & the formula concept the inversion: from loop to identity launch geometry: ceil-divide, then guard 2d grids: images get coordinates grid-stride loops: one launch shape for any n worked example key points quiz exercises 1. what >> actually passes (and how scalars travel) 2. index dialects you'll read in the wild 3. gotchas scenario — the review you can now give practice saxpy on a million floats, for real. the kernel body you wrote in 3.4, the launch arithmetic from m1 l2, the guard from m2 l2, the hardware from 4.4 — today they compile together. one new skill: thinking \"my index\" instead of \"the loop.\""},{"url":"module-5/lesson-03.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.3 Memory Management","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","Two address spaces, one pointer type","The lifecycle: the 3.5 dance, six steps","Asynchrony: the CPU runs ahead","Managed memory: the honest tool","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The allocation family (when each earns its place)","2. cudaMemcpy2D and strided reality","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the harness that made week two boring (in the best way)","Practice"],"summary":"3.5 made you the garbage collector; today you manage TWO heaps separated by 4.6's bridge. The rules are ones you already keep — one owner, exact frees, bytes-not-elements — plus one new law: know which world every pointer belongs to.","text":"5.3 memory management memory management — two worlds, one bridge, your discipline concept two address spaces, one pointer type the lifecycle: the 3.5 dance, six steps asynchrony: the cpu runs ahead managed memory: the honest tool worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the allocation family (when each earns its place) 2. cudamemcpy2d and strided reality 3. gotchas scenario — the harness that made week two boring (in the best way) practice 3.5 made you the garbage collector; today you manage two heaps separated by 4.6's bridge. the rules are ones you already keep — one owner, exact frees, bytes-not-elements — plus one new law: know which world every pointer belongs to."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-04.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.4 Errors & compute-sanitizer","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","Two channels, two moments","Sticky vs non-sticky: which errors kill the patient","The discipline: CHECK everything, bracket launches","compute-sanitizer: 3.8's alarm panel, GPU edition","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The error taxonomy you'll actually meet","2. printf and assert inside kernels","3. cuda-gdb, in one honest paragraph","4. Gotchas","Scenario — the 40-minute production fire, replayed with this lesson"],"summary":"3.8 armed your CPU code with gdb, ASan, UBSan. The GPU needs its own alarm panel, because 5.3's asynchrony adds a twist C never had: the crash happens lines after the crime. Today you learn where errors actually surface, which ones kill the context, and the sanitizer that names the guilty thread.","text":"5.4 errors & compute-sanitizer error handling & compute-sanitizer — alarms for a machine that fails later concept two channels, two moments sticky vs non-sticky: which errors kill the patient the discipline: check everything, bracket launches compute-sanitizer: 3.8's alarm panel, gpu edition worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the error taxonomy you'll actually meet 2. printf and assert inside kernels 3. cuda-gdb, in one honest paragraph 4. gotchas scenario — the 40-minute production fire, replayed with this lesson practice 3.8 armed your cpu code with gdb, asan, ubsan. the gpu needs its own alarm panel, because 5.3's asynchrony adds a twist c never had: the crash happens lines after the crime. today you learn where errors actually surface, which ones kill the context, and the sanitizer that names the guilty thread."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-05.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.5 Shared Memory & Tiling","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","__shared__: per-block scratchpad, explicitly yours","Why it exists: reuse, the roofline lever","__syncthreads(): the barrier that makes sharing safe","The tiled-loop skeleton","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Dynamic shared memory (size decided at launch)","2. When shared memory does NOT pay","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the inherited kernel and the one-line diagnosis","Practice"],"summary":"CPU caches guess what to keep (4.5's LRU bets). The GPU hands you the SRAM and says: you decide. Today you take 4.2's blocking trick, move it on-chip, and watch matmul's arithmetic intensity climb the roofline exactly as 4.7 predicted. One new keyword, one new barrier, one old idea.","text":"5.5 shared memory & tiling shared memory & tiling — the programmable cache you've been promised since 4.5 concept __shared__: per-block scratchpad, explicitly yours why it exists: reuse, the roofline lever __syncthreads(): the barrier that makes sharing safe the tiled-loop skeleton worked example key points quiz exercises 1. dynamic shared memory (size decided at launch) 2. when shared memory does not pay 3. gotchas scenario — the inherited kernel and the one-line diagnosis practice cpu caches guess what to keep (4.5's lru bets). the gpu hands you the sram and says: you decide. today you take 4.2's blocking trick, move it on-chip, and watch matmul's arithmetic intensity climb the roofline exactly as 4.7 predicted. one new keyword, one new barrier, one old idea."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-06.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.6 Warps, Divergence & Occupancy","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","Your block, sliced into warps","Divergence: the if that runs both sides","Occupancy: the dial, formalized","Registers, and the spill cliff","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Post-Volta reality: warps are not your sync primitive","2. Branches the compiler already flattened","3. The occupancy API (let the runtime do your table)","4. Gotchas","Scenario — the flag that optimized one kernel and taxed eleven"],"summary":"4.4 told you threads execute 32 at a time on one instruction stream. Today that fact starts editing your code: every if gets priced, every register counted, and \"how many warps are resident\" becomes a number you compute before the profiler confirms it.","text":"5.6 warps, divergence & occupancy warps, divergence & occupancy — what the hardware does with your ifs concept your block, sliced into warps divergence: the if that runs both sides occupancy: the dial, formalized registers, and the spill cliff worked example key points quiz exercises 1. post-volta reality: warps are not your sync primitive 2. branches the compiler already flattened 3. the occupancy api (let the runtime do your table) 4. gotchas scenario — the flag that optimized one kernel and taxed eleven practice 4.4 told you threads execute 32 at a time on one instruction stream. today that fact starts editing your code: every if gets priced, every register counted, and \"how many warps are resident\" becomes a number you compute before the profiler confirms it."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-07.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.7 Coalescing in Code","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","Read the subscript, see the transactions","The stride cliff, priced in advance","AoS → SoA: the fix that lives outside the kernel","float4: four elements, one instruction","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Sectors, requests, and the metric that names the crime","2. When gathers are legal: caches as coalescing insurance","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the one-line diff worth 11×","Practice"],"summary":"4.5 gave you the physics: a warp's 32 addresses become memory transactions, and scattered addresses buy wasted sectors. Today the physics moves into your index arithmetic — you'll learn to read a subscript and see the bus traffic it orders, then fix the three classic crimes: strides, AoS, and the transpose.","text":"5.7 coalescing in code coalescing in code — the index formula is the memory system concept read the subscript, see the transactions the stride cliff, priced in advance aos → soa: the fix that lives outside the kernel float4: four elements, one instruction worked example key points quiz exercises 1. sectors, requests, and the metric that names the crime 2. when gathers are legal: caches as coalescing insurance 3. gotchas scenario — the one-line diff worth 11× practice 4.5 gave you the physics: a warp's 32 addresses become memory transactions, and scattered addresses buy wasted sectors. today the physics moves into your index arithmetic — you'll learn to read a subscript and see the bus traffic it orders, then fix the three classic crimes: strides, aos, and the transpose."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-08.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.8 Reductions & Atomics","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 8","heads":["Concept","The race, and the atomic","The privatization ladder — same trick at every scale","The block tree, done right","The last 32: shuffles, not shared","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The atomic family (and the gaps)","2. atomicMax for float — the M1 L8 party trick, weaponized","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the week-long hunt for a ghost that was an atomicAdd","Practice"],"summary":"Everything so far kept threads apart: own index, own element, no sharing (5.2's law). But sum, max, argmin, histogram — half of real computing — need threads to MEET. Today: the race you'd write naively, the atomic that fixes it, the contention bill it runs up, and the ladder of privatization that makes reductions run ","text":"5.8 reductions & atomics reductions & atomics — when 100,000 threads want the same variable concept the race, and the atomic the privatization ladder — same trick at every scale the block tree, done right the last 32: shuffles, not shared worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the atomic family (and the gaps) 2. atomicmax for float — the m1 l8 party trick, weaponized 3. gotchas scenario — the week-long hunt for a ghost that was an atomicadd practice everything so far kept threads apart: own index, own element, no sharing (5.2's law). but sum, max, argmin, histogram — half of real computing — need threads to meet. today: the race you'd write naively, the atomic that fixes it, the contention bill it runs up, and the ladder of privatization that makes reductions run at memcpy speed."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-09.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.9 Streams, Events & Async","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 9","heads":["Concept","Streams: ordered lanes, independent lanes","Pinned memory: the price of admission","Events: timestamps and cross-lane dependencies","The pipeline: 5.3's pool + streams + a ring","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The default stream's two personalities","2. Callbacks, graphs, and what comes after (honest previews)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the one-line log that doubled p99","Practice"],"summary":"Your GPU has been idling through this whole module: while a kernel computed, the copy engines sat parked; while data crossed the bridge, the SMs did nothing. 4.1's pipeline lesson said overlap is how hardware earns its keep — today you get the steering wheel: streams to order work, events to time and chain it, pinned m","text":"5.9 streams, events & async streams, events & async — three engines, and the timeline you now command concept streams: ordered lanes, independent lanes pinned memory: the price of admission events: timestamps and cross-lane dependencies the pipeline: 5.3's pool + streams + a ring worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the default stream's two personalities 2. callbacks, graphs, and what comes after (honest previews) 3. gotchas scenario — the one-line log that doubled p99 practice your gpu has been idling through this whole module: while a kernel computed, the copy engines sat parked; while data crossed the bridge, the sms did nothing. 4.1's pipeline lesson said overlap is how hardware earns its keep — today you get the steering wheel: streams to order work, events to time and chain it, pinned memory to unlock the dma engines."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-10.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.10 Nsight Profiling","num":"Module 5 · Lesson 10","heads":["Concept","The division of labor — never confuse the instruments","Reading an nsys timeline like a native","Reading an ncu report: SOL first, always","Profiling hygiene (numbers you can defend)","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The metric→lesson dictionary (your triage card)","2. Profile-guided CI (the regression tripwire)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the month of tiling that should have been an afternoon of streams","Practice"],"summary":"You've been optimizing by MODEL all module: rooflines predicted, tables audited, measurements confirmed. The profilers close the triangle — nsys answers where does the time go (the timeline), ncu answers why is this kernel slow (the counters). Today they stop being previews and become your daily loop.","text":"5.10 nsight profiling nsight profiling — two instruments, one loop, zero guessing concept the division of labor — never confuse the instruments reading an nsys timeline like a native reading an ncu report: sol first, always profiling hygiene (numbers you can defend) worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the metric→lesson dictionary (your triage card) 2. profile-guided ci (the regression tripwire) 3. gotchas scenario — the month of tiling that should have been an afternoon of streams practice you've been optimizing by model all module: rooflines predicted, tables audited, measurements confirmed. the profilers close the triangle — nsys answers where does the time go (the timeline), ncu answers why is this kernel slow (the counters). today they stop being previews and become your daily loop."},{"url":"module-5/lesson-11.html","kind":"core","group":"Core","title":"5.11 Capstone — The Matmul Ladder","num":"Capstone","heads":["The spec","The ladder at altitude — know the terrain before climbing","Phase 0 — the trust stack (build this BEFORE any kernel)","Phases 1–5 — the climb (one rung, one lever, one audit)","Phase 6 — the ship package","Key points","Defense quiz — the two you WILL be asked","The build — acceptance criteria per phase","1. What the next 1.4× costs (so you can cite it, not chase it)","2. Gotchas (capstone-specific, all bloodied by predecessors)","3. Scenario — forty-five minutes, one repo, hired","Defense bank — answer all seven in writing, in your own words"],"summary":"No new concepts today. The capstone is a PROJECT: climb matmul from 2 GFLOP/s to ~40% of your GPU's peak, one audited rung at a time, and package it so a stranger — or an interviewer — can verify every claim. This repo is the deliverable of the entire core curriculum. Build it like it's going on your résumé, because it","text":"5.11 capstone — the matmul ladder capstone — the matmul ladder: everything you know, one repo, defensible the spec the ladder at altitude — know the terrain before climbing phase 0 — the trust stack (build this before any kernel) phases 1–5 — the climb (one rung, one lever, one audit) phase 6 — the ship package key points defense quiz — the two you will be asked the build — acceptance criteria per phase 1. what the next 1.4× costs (so you can cite it, not chase it) 2. gotchas (capstone-specific, all bloodied by predecessors) 3. scenario — forty-five minutes, one repo, hired defense bank — answer all seven in writing, in your own words no new concepts today. the capstone is a project: climb matmul from 2 gflop/s to ~40% of your gpu's peak, one audited rung at a time, and package it so a stranger — or an interviewer — can verify every claim. this repo is the deliverable of the entire core curriculum. build it like it's going on your résumé, because it is."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-01.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A1 Calculus for Backprop","num":"Track A · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","A derivative is a local exchange rate","The chain rule is bookkeeping, not magic","Gradients: all the knobs at once","Backprop = the chain rule on a graph, with memoization","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Jacobians, VJPs, and why we go BACKWARD","2. Kinks, plateaus, and \"differentiable enough\"","3. What backward costs: the memory bill","4. Gotchas","Scenario — the model that trained \"fine\" on a wrong gradient"],"summary":"The core made you fast at running math on GPUs; Track A is about the math that TRAINS models — and it is one idea, taught in school, industrialized: the slope. This lesson takes you from \"what's a derivative\" to \"I can derive and verify backprop by hand,\" because every kernel you'll write in this track exists to move g","text":"a1 calculus for backprop calculus for backprop — from slopes to the algorithm that trains everything concept a derivative is a local exchange rate the chain rule is bookkeeping, not magic gradients: all the knobs at once backprop = the chain rule on a graph, with memoization worked example key points quiz exercises 1. jacobians, vjps, and why we go backward 2. kinks, plateaus, and \"differentiable enough\" 3. what backward costs: the memory bill 4. gotchas scenario — the model that trained \"fine\" on a wrong gradient practice the core made you fast at running math on gpus; track a is about the math that trains models — and it is one idea, taught in school, industrialized: the slope. this lesson takes you from \"what's a derivative\" to \"i can derive and verify backprop by hand,\" because every kernel you'll write in this track exists to move gradients."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-02.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A2 PyTorch Internals","num":"Track A · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","A Tensor is a header pointing at bytes — M2 L5, verbatim","Autograd: A1's graph, recorded live","The dispatcher: one op name, a table of kernels","CUDA semantics: 5.3's queue wearing a Python costume","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Leaves, .grad, and who gets gradients","2. requires_grad vs no_grad vs inference_mode (the recording dial)","3. torch.profiler: 5.10's loop, in-process","4. Gotchas","Scenario — \"the GPU is only 60% busy\" (a torch remix of old physics)"],"summary":"PyTorch (2.11 at this writing) will spend the rest of Track A pretending to be magic. This lesson strips it: a Tensor is M2 L5's strides model, autograd is A1's graph as a recorded tape, CUDA calls are 5.3's async queue, and every \"weird PyTorch behavior\" you'll ever debug is one of those three leaking through.","text":"a2 pytorch internals pytorch internals — you already know this machine; here are its new names concept a tensor is a header pointing at bytes — m2 l5, verbatim autograd: a1's graph, recorded live the dispatcher: one op name, a table of kernels cuda semantics: 5.3's queue wearing a python costume worked example key points quiz exercises 1. leaves, .grad, and who gets gradients 2. requires_grad vs no_grad vs inference_mode (the recording dial) 3. torch.profiler: 5.10's loop, in-process 4. gotchas scenario — \"the gpu is only 60% busy\" (a torch remix of old physics) practice pytorch (2.11 at this writing) will spend the rest of track a pretending to be magic. this lesson strips it: a tensor is m2 l5's strides model, autograd is a1's graph as a recorded tape, cuda calls are 5.3's async queue, and every \"weird pytorch behavior\" you'll ever debug is one of those three leaking through."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-03.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A3 Custom CUDA Ops for PyTorch","num":"Track A · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","The journey of a call","Joining autograd: an op must declare its VJP","The contracts, and who they protect","The fixture pyramid — an op is its test suite","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Dispatch and accumulate-type, the two-line house style","2. torch.library and the compile-era contract (A9 preview)","3. Ownership across the boundary (3.5's tables, one last time)","4. Gotchas","Scenario — the op library that made a platform team fast, then famous"],"summary":"Everything below the binding is Module 5 unchanged. This lesson is about the uniform: the contracts a kernel must sign to live inside autograd (shapes, dtypes, contiguity, streams, a backward), and the fixtures that make it trustworthy. It's short on new ideas and long on discipline — which is exactly what production o","text":"a3 custom cuda ops for pytorch custom cuda ops — your module 5 kernels, wearing a torch uniform concept the journey of a call joining autograd: an op must declare its vjp the contracts, and who they protect the fixture pyramid — an op is its test suite worked example key points quiz exercises 1. dispatch and accumulate-type, the two-line house style 2. torch.library and the compile-era contract (a9 preview) 3. ownership across the boundary (3.5's tables, one last time) 4. gotchas scenario — the op library that made a platform team fast, then famous practice everything below the binding is module 5 unchanged. this lesson is about the uniform: the contracts a kernel must sign to live inside autograd (shapes, dtypes, contiguity, streams, a backward), and the fixtures that make it trustworthy. it's short on new ideas and long on discipline — which is exactly what production op libraries are."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-04.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A4 Triton Kernels in Python","num":"Track A · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","The program model: one PROGRAM = one tile","Masks: the guard, the zero-fill, and the identity element","Autotuning: 5.6's knob ritual, mechanized","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The lowering pipeline (and which decisions stay yours)","2. tl.dot and the matmul skeleton (5.11 in twenty lines)","3. When Triton, when CUDA (the honest boundary, 2026)","4. Gotchas","Scenario — five attention variants in the time CUDA budgeted for one","Practice"],"summary":"Triton (3.x, the language inside torch.compile) moves your unit of authorship from the THREAD to the TILE: you write what one block computes; the compiler picks threads, vector widths, and staging. Module 5's cost model survives intact — coalescing, occupancy, and identity elements are still YOUR job. That's why you le","text":"a4 triton kernels in python triton — kernels at the tile level, in python, without surrendering the physics concept the program model: one program = one tile masks: the guard, the zero-fill, and the identity element autotuning: 5.6's knob ritual, mechanized worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the lowering pipeline (and which decisions stay yours) 2. tl.dot and the matmul skeleton (5.11 in twenty lines) 3. when triton, when cuda (the honest boundary, 2026) 4. gotchas scenario — five attention variants in the time cuda budgeted for one practice triton (3.x, the language inside torch.compile) moves your unit of authorship from the thread to the tile: you write what one block computes; the compiler picks threads, vector widths, and staging. module 5's cost model survives intact — coalescing, occupancy, and identity elements are still your job. that's why you learned cuda first, and why you'll now be fast in triton instead of lost in it."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-05.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A5 ML Kernel Patterns — Fusion, Softmax, FlashAttention","num":"Track A · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","The traffic ledger: where a \"fast\" model leaks","Online softmax: the identity that makes streaming legal","LayerNorm: the cancellation trap wearing a normalization costume","FlashAttention: 5.5's tiling + the online identity = O(N) traffic","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. What fuses, what can't, and why (the boundary rules)","2. Flash backward, honestly (where the memory went)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the fused kernel that was right about the wrong model","Practice"],"summary":"Every famous ML kernel of the last decade is one idea executed well: stop driving tensors to HBM between steps. This lesson builds that idea from the softmax traffic ledger up to a from-scratch FlashAttention you'll verify to machine epsilon — and by the end, \"we should fuse this\" will be an arithmetic conclusion you c","text":"a5 ml kernel patterns — fusion, softmax, flashattention ml kernel patterns — fusion is the whole game, flashattention is its masterpiece concept the traffic ledger: where a \"fast\" model leaks online softmax: the identity that makes streaming legal layernorm: the cancellation trap wearing a normalization costume flashattention: 5.5's tiling + the online identity = o(n) traffic worked example key points quiz exercises 1. what fuses, what can't, and why (the boundary rules) 2. flash backward, honestly (where the memory went) 3. gotchas scenario — the fused kernel that was right about the wrong model practice every famous ml kernel of the last decade is one idea executed well: stop driving tensors to hbm between steps. this lesson builds that idea from the softmax traffic ledger up to a from-scratch flashattention you'll verify to machine epsilon — and by the end, \"we should fuse this\" will be an arithmetic conclusion you compute, not a vibe you inherit."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-06.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A6 Mixed Precision & Tensor Cores","num":"Track A · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","The format menu: range vs precision, on one axis","The two failure modes, measured","The mixed-precision recipe (what torch.amp actually does)","Tensor cores: the units your dtypes unlock","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. fp8 and the scaling-factor era (Hopper/Blackwell)","2. Stochastic rounding (why fp8-era optimizers survive)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the 4 a.m. inf storm and the two-knob confusion","Practice"],"summary":"Halving your dtype halves every byte on 4.6's ledger and unlocks the matrix units that do most of a modern GPU's work. The price is paid in M1 L8's currency — range and precision — and this lesson makes every line of that price list MEASURED: where fp16 flushes to zero, where sums silently stall at 4% of their true val","text":"a6 mixed precision & tensor cores mixed precision & tensor cores — buying speed with bits, without buying regret concept the format menu: range vs precision, on one axis the two failure modes, measured the mixed-precision recipe (what torch.amp actually does) tensor cores: the units your dtypes unlock worked example key points quiz exercises 1. fp8 and the scaling-factor era (hopper/blackwell) 2. stochastic rounding (why fp8-era optimizers survive) 3. gotchas scenario — the 4 a.m. inf storm and the two-knob confusion practice halving your dtype halves every byte on 4.6's ledger and unlocks the matrix units that do most of a modern gpu's work. the price is paid in m1 l8's currency — range and precision — and this lesson makes every line of that price list measured: where fp16 flushes to zero, where sums silently stall at 4% of their true value, and which shapes the tensor cores refuse to eat."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-07.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A7 Multi-GPU — NCCL, DDP, FSDP","num":"Track A · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","The primitive: allreduce is 5.8 across machines","Ring allreduce: derived, then verified by simulation","DDP: hide the allreduce under backward","FSDP: shard the STATE, gather on demand","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Collectives beyond allreduce (the vocabulary)","2. Determinism across ranks (5.8's registry, sharded)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the 64-GPU purchase that bought 11%","Practice"],"summary":"Data-parallel training is 5.8's reduction with network cables for lanes: every GPU computes gradients on its shard, and an allreduce averages them. This lesson derives the ring algorithm (and verifies it by simulation), prices DDP's overlap and FSDP's memory trade with real arithmetic, and hands you the one law that de","text":"a7 multi-gpu — nccl, ddp, fsdp multi-gpu — the σ leaves the building concept the primitive: allreduce is 5.8 across machines ring allreduce: derived, then verified by simulation ddp: hide the allreduce under backward fsdp: shard the state, gather on demand worked example key points quiz exercises 1. collectives beyond allreduce (the vocabulary) 2. determinism across ranks (5.8's registry, sharded) 3. gotchas scenario — the 64-gpu purchase that bought 11% practice data-parallel training is 5.8's reduction with network cables for lanes: every gpu computes gradients on its shard, and an allreduce averages them. this lesson derives the ring algorithm (and verifies it by simulation), prices ddp's overlap and fsdp's memory trade with real arithmetic, and hands you the one law that decides every scaling question: compute the comm lane and the compute lane; the max rules (5.9)."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-08.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A8 Inference — Quantization, KV-Cache, Batching, vLLM","num":"Track A · Lesson 8","heads":["Concept","The decode loop's brutal economics","The KV-cache: the tensor that decides your batch size","Paged KV + continuous batching: an OS course crashes the party","Quantization: shrink bytes-per-token, pay in ulps","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Speculative decoding (beating the mat·vec tax with a draft)","2. The latency SLO zoo (what \"fast\" means is a product decision)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the SKU that lost money on its best customers","Practice"],"summary":"Training amortizes; serving pays retail. Every generated token re-reads the entire model, every conversation drags a growing cache, and every idle microsecond is money. This lesson gives you the four load-bearing ideas — the KV ledger, paged memory, continuous batching, quantization — each with arithmetic you'll verify","text":"a8 inference — quantization, kv-cache, batching, vllm inference — serving is a memory-bandwidth business wearing an ai costume concept the decode loop's brutal economics the kv-cache: the tensor that decides your batch size paged kv + continuous batching: an os course crashes the party quantization: shrink bytes-per-token, pay in ulps worked example key points quiz exercises 1. speculative decoding (beating the mat·vec tax with a draft) 2. the latency slo zoo (what \"fast\" means is a product decision) 3. gotchas scenario — the sku that lost money on its best customers practice training amortizes; serving pays retail. every generated token re-reads the entire model, every conversation drags a growing cache, and every idle microsecond is money. this lesson gives you the four load-bearing ideas — the kv ledger, paged memory, continuous batching, quantization — each with arithmetic you'll verify, ending in the serving-envelope calculation that is the ml-infra interview."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-09.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A9 torch.compile & TorchInductor","num":"Track A · Lesson 9","heads":["Concept","The stack: capture → differentiate → generate","Graph breaks: where capture surrenders","Guards and recompilation: the cache you didn't know you had","When it wins (the table you already derived)","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. CUDA graphs via reduce-overhead (5.9 §2, productized)","2. AOTInductor & the deployment boundary (A10's door)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the 30% win that came with a 40-minute time bomb","Practice"],"summary":"torch.compile captures your Python model into graphs, fuses the glue, and emits Triton — literally A4's language, written by a machine applying A5's ledger. This lesson teaches you to operate that machine: what breaks its capture, when its wins are real, why it recompiles, and how to read the kernels it writes — becaus","text":"a9 torch.compile & torchinductor torch.compile — your kernel skills, automated; your job, moved up a level concept the stack: capture → differentiate → generate graph breaks: where capture surrenders guards and recompilation: the cache you didn't know you had when it wins (the table you already derived) worked example key points quiz exercises 1. cuda graphs via reduce-overhead (5.9 §2, productized) 2. aotinductor & the deployment boundary (a10's door) 3. gotchas scenario — the 30% win that came with a 40-minute time bomb practice torch.compile captures your python model into graphs, fuses the glue, and emits triton — literally a4's language, written by a machine applying a5's ledger. this lesson teaches you to operate that machine: what breaks its capture, when its wins are real, why it recompiles, and how to read the kernels it writes — because \"the compiler did something slow\" is only debuggable by someone who knows what it should have done."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-10.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A10 Deployment — Containers, Serving, Monitoring","num":"Track A · Lesson 10","heads":["Concept","Containers: the driver stays outside","The serving path: every hop is a place p99 goes to die","Metrics that lie, metrics that don't","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Sharing silicon: MIG and MPS (when one GPU serves many tenants)","2. The cost sheet (connecting A8's envelope to invoices)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the green dashboard and the silent 12%","Practice"],"summary":"Everything before this lesson made code fast; this lesson keeps it fast at 3 a.m., on 200 heterogeneous nodes, through driver upgrades and traffic spikes. The material is unglamorous and decisive: version invariants, warmup contracts, metrics that don't lie, and the discipline of treating every operational rule as a me","text":"a10 deployment — containers, serving, monitoring deployment — where kernels meet pagers concept containers: the driver stays outside the serving path: every hop is a place p99 goes to die metrics that lie, metrics that don't worked example key points quiz exercises 1. sharing silicon: mig and mps (when one gpu serves many tenants) 2. the cost sheet (connecting a8's envelope to invoices) 3. gotchas scenario — the green dashboard and the silent 12% practice everything before this lesson made code fast; this lesson keeps it fast at 3 a.m., on 200 heterogeneous nodes, through driver upgrades and traffic spikes. the material is unglamorous and decisive: version invariants, warmup contracts, metrics that don't lie, and the discipline of treating every operational rule as a mechanism you can cite — because in production, superstition scales worse than anything."},{"url":"track-a/lesson-11.html","kind":"track-a","group":"Track A · ML/AI Infra","title":"A11 Capstone — The Fused Epilogue, Shipped","num":"Capstone","heads":["The spec","The ladder and its honest terrain","Phase 0 — the trust stack (build before the kernel, as always)","Phases 1–4 — the climb","Phase 5 — serve it (r4)","Key points","Defense quiz — the two you WILL be asked","The build — acceptance criteria","1. The natural extensions (each a weekend, each interview-visible)","2. Gotchas (capstone-specific, pre-bled)","Scenario — forty-five minutes, one repo, the offer","Defense bank — answer all seven in writing before any interview"],"summary":"The project: fuse bias+GELU into the GEMM itself — the one fusion torch.compile structurally can't do (it delegates GEMMs to cuBLAS and fuses AROUND them, A9) — then prove it, profile it, wrap it in autograd, and serve it behind an endpoint with honest dashboards. Small enough for three days; complete enough that every","text":"a11 capstone — the fused epilogue, shipped capstone — a fused matmul epilogue, from triton to a served endpoint the spec the ladder and its honest terrain phase 0 — the trust stack (build before the kernel, as always) phases 1–4 — the climb phase 5 — serve it (r4) key points defense quiz — the two you will be asked the build — acceptance criteria 1. the natural extensions (each a weekend, each interview-visible) 2. gotchas (capstone-specific, pre-bled) scenario — forty-five minutes, one repo, the offer defense bank — answer all seven in writing before any interview the project: fuse bias+gelu into the gemm itself — the one fusion torch.compile structurally can't do (it delegates gemms to cublas and fuses around them, a9) — then prove it, profile it, wrap it in autograd, and serve it behind an endpoint with honest dashboards. small enough for three days; complete enough that every track a lesson appears in the repo. this is your ml-infra portfolio piece; 5.11 was its ancestor."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-01.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B1 Numerical Calculus from Scratch","num":"Track B · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","Derivatives from neighbors: finite differences","The V-shape again — now it sets your step size","Integrating ODEs: order buys digits","Stability is a SEPARATE contract","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Stencil coefficients to order — the general recipe","2. Stiffness and implicit methods, priced","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the six-week \"GPU bug\" that was a 60-second audit","Practice"],"summary":"Track B computes PHYSICAL quantities: heat, orbits, prices, fields. The calculus is school-level; the craft is knowing each method's ERROR LAW and STABILITY CONTRACT — and in this track we don't cite those laws, we measure them. Every order, wall, and drift below was executed for this lesson; you'll re-measure them all","text":"b1 numerical calculus from scratch numerical calculus — the math of simulation, taught by experiment concept derivatives from neighbors: finite differences the v-shape again — now it sets your step size integrating odes: order buys digits stability is a separate contract worked example key points quiz exercises 1. stencil coefficients to order — the general recipe 2. stiffness and implicit methods, priced 3. gotchas scenario — the six-week \"gpu bug\" that was a 60-second audit practice track b computes physical quantities: heat, orbits, prices, fields. the calculus is school-level; the craft is knowing each method's error law and stability contract — and in this track we don't cite those laws, we measure them. every order, wall, and drift below was executed for this lesson; you'll re-measure them all in the exercises."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-02.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B2 cuBLAS, cuSPARSE & cuSOLVER","num":"Track B · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","The ladder, and where your roofline literacy plugs in","Column-major: the identity, not the copy","Sparse: a format decision wearing a math costume","cuSOLVER: factor once, solve many — and know your family","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Handles, streams, workspaces (the library citizenship rules)","2. Condition number: the health metric the library won't check","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the week the \"wrong physics\" was a leading dimension","Practice"],"summary":"Science codes live for decades; hand kernels rot in years (A3's scenario). So Track B's law is library-first — but the LIBRARY doesn't choose your storage format, your solver family, or your layout convention, and those decisions dominate performance by 10–100×. This lesson is about the decisions: dense vs sparse (meas","text":"b2 cublas, cusparse & cusolver cublas, cusparse, cusolver — buy the linear algebra, own the decisions concept the ladder, and where your roofline literacy plugs in column-major: the identity, not the copy sparse: a format decision wearing a math costume cusolver: factor once, solve many — and know your family worked example key points quiz exercises 1. handles, streams, workspaces (the library citizenship rules) 2. condition number: the health metric the library won't check 3. gotchas scenario — the week the \"wrong physics\" was a leading dimension practice science codes live for decades; hand kernels rot in years (a3's scenario). so track b's law is library-first — but the library doesn't choose your storage format, your solver family, or your layout convention, and those decisions dominate performance by 10–100×. this lesson is about the decisions: dense vs sparse (measured crossover included), direct vs iterative, and the column-major identity that saves you from the classic first-week disaster."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-03.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B3 Stencils & Grid Codes","num":"Track B · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","The 5-point stencil: B1's second derivative, twice","The CFL wall — B1's stability contract in 2D, with a fuse","Halos: the parallel structure the stencil forces","Physics-supplied fixtures (the cheat codes, executed)","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Boundaries: where correctness goes to die","2. Beyond heat: the same skeleton, harder physics","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the seam that published, almost","Practice"],"summary":"Half of simulation is a grid where every point updates from its neighbors — heat, waves, fluids, fields. One pattern, one memory story (bandwidth, always), one parallel story (tiles + halos), and a set of physics-supplied fixtures so good they feel like cheating. Everything below is executed: conservation to 2e-16, the","text":"b3 stencils & grid codes stencils & grid codes — the workhorse pattern of computational science concept the 5-point stencil: b1's second derivative, twice the cfl wall — b1's stability contract in 2d, with a fuse halos: the parallel structure the stencil forces physics-supplied fixtures (the cheat codes, executed) worked example key points quiz exercises 1. boundaries: where correctness goes to die 2. beyond heat: the same skeleton, harder physics 3. gotchas scenario — the seam that published, almost practice half of simulation is a grid where every point updates from its neighbors — heat, waves, fluids, fields. one pattern, one memory story (bandwidth, always), one parallel story (tiles + halos), and a set of physics-supplied fixtures so good they feel like cheating. everything below is executed: conservation to 2e-16, the spread law to four digits, and a stability wall that hides for 200 steps before detonating at step 2000."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-04.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B4 Monte Carlo & cuRAND","num":"Track B · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","The law: 1/√N, measured — including a trap about measuring it","Parallel randomness: independence by construction","Variance reduction: algebra as a sample multiplier","Why GPUs eat Monte Carlo alive","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. From uniforms to everything (the transformation toolbox)","2. Error bars in production (the part beyond ±1.96σ)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the pricing desk that bought algebra instead of a cluster","Practice"],"summary":"When integrals live in 100 dimensions (finance, particle transport, statistical physics), grids die exponentially and random sampling doesn't care. The price is the slowest convergence law in this course — error ∝ 1/√N, measured below at slope −0.494 — which makes Monte Carlo simultaneously the most GPU-hungry workload","text":"b4 monte carlo & curand monte carlo — the method that trades certainty for dimensions, at 1/√n concept the law: 1/√n, measured — including a trap about measuring it parallel randomness: independence by construction variance reduction: algebra as a sample multiplier why gpus eat monte carlo alive worked example key points quiz exercises 1. from uniforms to everything (the transformation toolbox) 2. error bars in production (the part beyond ±1.96σ) 3. gotchas scenario — the pricing desk that bought algebra instead of a cluster practice when integrals live in 100 dimensions (finance, particle transport, statistical physics), grids die exponentially and random sampling doesn't care. the price is the slowest convergence law in this course — error ∝ 1/√n, measured below at slope −0.494 — which makes monte carlo simultaneously the most gpu-hungry workload in science and the field where algebra (variance reduction, measured at 62×) beats hardware hardest. plus the parallel-randomness problem curand solved properly, and folklore didn't."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-05.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B5 MPI + CUDA, NCCL & GPUDirect","num":"Track B · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","The rank model: SPMD, private worlds","CUDA-aware MPI and GPUDirect: device pointers on the wire","The halo exchange, distributed — and its two-lane audit","NCCL vs MPI: two tools, one app","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Nonblocking semantics: the contract, precisely","2. Communicators, topology, and placement","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the \"MPI is slow on GPUs\" verdict, overturned in an afternoon","Practice"],"summary":"MPI is 5.3's discipline at datacenter scale: N processes, N private memories, and NOTHING moves unless you say so. This lesson gives you the rank model, the CUDA-aware path (and how to verify you're actually ON it), the halo exchange your B3 harness was built for — and the two-lane arithmetic that predicted a 99%-vs-50","text":"b5 mpi + cuda, nccl & gpudirect mpi + cuda — many machines, explicit messages, and the audit that predicts scaling concept the rank model: spmd, private worlds cuda-aware mpi and gpudirect: device pointers on the wire the halo exchange, distributed — and its two-lane audit nccl vs mpi: two tools, one app worked example key points quiz exercises 1. nonblocking semantics: the contract, precisely 2. communicators, topology, and placement 3. gotchas scenario — the \"mpi is slow on gpus\" verdict, overturned in an afternoon practice mpi is 5.3's discipline at datacenter scale: n processes, n private memories, and nothing moves unless you say so. this lesson gives you the rank model, the cuda-aware path (and how to verify you're actually on it), the halo exchange your b3 harness was built for — and the two-lane arithmetic that predicted a 99%-vs-50% scaling split before any cluster time was burned."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-06.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B6 OpenMP & OpenACC Offload","num":"Track B · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","The model: annotate loops, compiler emits the machinery","Data clauses ARE the ownership table","The 90/10 curve — where directives end","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Unstructured data + deep structures (real codes are messy)","2. Kernel-shape control (when the defaults disappoint)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the 300k-line code that made it to the GPU era in one quarter","Practice"],"summary":"Real science codes are decades of Fortran and C that will NEVER be rewritten in CUDA — and don't need to be. Directives (OpenACC, OpenMP target) let the compiler write 5.3's plumbing and 5.2's kernels from annotations, reaching 80–90% of hand-tuned speed on bandwidth-bound physics for ~1% of the code churn. The craft i","text":"b6 openmp & openacc offload directives — porting 300,000 lines of science without rewriting them concept the model: annotate loops, compiler emits the machinery data clauses are the ownership table the 90/10 curve — where directives end worked example key points quiz exercises 1. unstructured data + deep structures (real codes are messy) 2. kernel-shape control (when the defaults disappoint) 3. gotchas scenario — the 300k-line code that made it to the gpu era in one quarter practice real science codes are decades of fortran and c that will never be rewritten in cuda — and don't need to be. directives (openacc, openmp target) let the compiler write 5.3's plumbing and 5.2's kernels from annotations, reaching 80–90% of hand-tuned speed on bandwidth-bound physics for ~1% of the code churn. the craft is entirely in the data clauses — 3.5's ownership tables as pragmas — and in knowing where the 90% ends."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-07.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B7 Clusters — SLURM, Modules, Apptainer","num":"Track B · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","A job's life","Queue economics: ask precisely","Environments: modules vs Apptainer","Filesystems and the checkpoint formula","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Checkpoint engineering (beyond the formula)","2. Login nodes, admins, and the social layer","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the allocation that almost died of politeness","Practice"],"summary":"A cluster is a shared machine with a queue, a filesystem hierarchy, and a walltime guillotine. The skills are unglamorous and career-critical: ask the scheduler precisely, freeze your environment reproducibly, write to the right filesystem, and checkpoint on a formula instead of a feeling — because at 512 ranks × 40 ho","text":"b7 clusters — slurm, modules, apptainer clusters — the etiquette, economics, and survival kit of shared supercomputers concept a job's life queue economics: ask precisely environments: modules vs apptainer filesystems and the checkpoint formula worked example key points quiz exercises 1. checkpoint engineering (beyond the formula) 2. login nodes, admins, and the social layer 3. gotchas scenario — the allocation that almost died of politeness practice a cluster is a shared machine with a queue, a filesystem hierarchy, and a walltime guillotine. the skills are unglamorous and career-critical: ask the scheduler precisely, freeze your environment reproducibly, write to the right filesystem, and checkpoint on a formula instead of a feeling — because at 512 ranks × 40 hours, failure isn't a risk, it's a schedule."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-08.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B8 Precision & Reproducibility","num":"Track B · Lesson 8","heads":["Concept","The summation ladder — all four rungs measured","The compiler can delete your algorithm — executed","Parallel statistics without tears: merge formulas","The reproducibility ladder — pick a rung in writing","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Where bits legitimately differ (the audit checklist)","2. Mixed-precision refinement (having both, honestly)","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the replication request that took twenty minutes","Practice"],"summary":"In ML, a wrong last bit is noise; in science, \"we couldn't reproduce it\" is a retraction. This lesson turns M1 L8's float literacy into professional machinery: a summation ladder measured across four rungs, a compiler flag shown DELETING an algorithm (Kahan's error reverting to exactly naive's under -ffast-math — execu","text":"b8 precision & reproducibility precision & reproducibility — the numerics contract science signs concept the summation ladder — all four rungs measured the compiler can delete your algorithm — executed parallel statistics without tears: merge formulas the reproducibility ladder — pick a rung in writing worked example key points quiz exercises 1. where bits legitimately differ (the audit checklist) 2. mixed-precision refinement (having both, honestly) 3. gotchas scenario — the replication request that took twenty minutes practice in ml, a wrong last bit is noise; in science, \"we couldn't reproduce it\" is a retraction. this lesson turns m1 l8's float literacy into professional machinery: a summation ladder measured across four rungs, a compiler flag shown deleting an algorithm (kahan's error reverting to exactly naive's under -ffast-math — executed), the cross-rank determinism story, and the reproducibility ladder your methods section must name a rung on."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-09.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B9 Thrust, CUB & cuFFT","num":"Track B · Lesson 9","heads":["Concept","The abstraction ladder","Scan: the primitive 5.8 couldn't build — and what it unlocks","cuFFT: plans, batches, and the 600× logarithm","Mathematics as the fixture supplier","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Thrust ergonomics that matter in real codes","2. CUB in your kernels: the composition pattern","3. Gotchas","Scenario — the plasma code that bought back its Tuesdays","Practice"],"summary":"You built reductions from warp shuffles (5.8) so that libraries would never be mysteries — and now you graduate to using them. Thrust for one-liners, CUB for in-kernel building blocks, and the primitive you COULDN'T build in 5.8's model — scan — delivered by the library that solved its impossible dependency. Plus cuFFT","text":"b9 thrust, cub & cufft thrust, cub & cufft — the primitives you stop hand-rolling today concept the abstraction ladder scan: the primitive 5.8 couldn't build — and what it unlocks cufft: plans, batches, and the 600× logarithm mathematics as the fixture supplier worked example key points quiz exercises 1. thrust ergonomics that matter in real codes 2. cub in your kernels: the composition pattern 3. gotchas scenario — the plasma code that bought back its tuesdays practice you built reductions from warp shuffles (5.8) so that libraries would never be mysteries — and now you graduate to using them. thrust for one-liners, cub for in-kernel building blocks, and the primitive you couldn't build in 5.8's model — scan — delivered by the library that solved its impossible dependency. plus cufft, where m1's logarithms cash out at a measured 600×, and mathematics itself supplies the test suite."},{"url":"track-b/lesson-10.html","kind":"track-b","group":"Track B · HPC","title":"B10 Capstone — The Scaling Study","num":"Capstone","heads":["The spec","The problem and its free oracles","The ladder","The report — the actual deliverable","Key points","Defense quiz — the two you WILL be asked","The build — acceptance criteria","1. Extensions that upgrade the repo (each a weekend)","2. Gotchas (capstone-specific, pre-bled)","3. Scenario — the defense, forty minutes, an allocation won","Defense bank — answer all seven in writing before any interview or committee"],"summary":"The B-track's thesis, executed end to end: a 2D heat solver taken from NumPy oracle to multi-node MPI+GPU, wrapped in physics fixtures, checkpointed on a formula, environment-recorded, and reported with labeled curves, an overlaid cost model, and a numerics contract. The deliverable is not speed — it's a REPORT a hosti","text":"b10 capstone — the scaling study capstone — one simulation, from serial oracle to a defensible scaling study the spec the problem and its free oracles the ladder the report — the actual deliverable key points defense quiz — the two you will be asked the build — acceptance criteria 1. extensions that upgrade the repo (each a weekend) 2. gotchas (capstone-specific, pre-bled) 3. scenario — the defense, forty minutes, an allocation won defense bank — answer all seven in writing before any interview or committee the b-track's thesis, executed end to end: a 2d heat solver taken from numpy oracle to multi-node mpi+gpu, wrapped in physics fixtures, checkpointed on a formula, environment-recorded, and reported with labeled curves, an overlaid cost model, and a numerics contract. the deliverable is not speed — it's a report a hostile reviewer can verify. that artifact is the hpc hiring bar, and you're about to own one."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-01.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C1 Graphics Math","num":"Track C · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","Homogeneous coordinates: why every point carries a 4th number","The perspective divide, by hand","Quaternions vs Euler angles: why rotation isn't three numbers","Normals need the inverse-transpose (and it's not obvious)","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The transform stack, one matrix at a time","2. Why 1/w is the magic quantity","3. Rotations: the representations and when to use each","Gotchas","Scenario — the shading bug that only appeared on Tuesdays"],"summary":"Track C is throughput engineering with a new consumer: an eyeball, not a fixture. A frame is a 16.6 ms budget (5.9's max-lane law, where the pipeline IS the GPU), and \"correct\" becomes \"measurably plausible\" — a reference image within a PSNR budget, not a bitwise match. It all rests on four pieces of linear algebra, an","text":"c1 graphics math graphics math — the coordinate machinery that puts pixels on screen concept homogeneous coordinates: why every point carries a 4th number the perspective divide, by hand quaternions vs euler angles: why rotation isn't three numbers normals need the inverse-transpose (and it's not obvious) worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the transform stack, one matrix at a time 2. why 1/w is the magic quantity 3. rotations: the representations and when to use each gotchas scenario — the shading bug that only appeared on tuesdays practice track c is throughput engineering with a new consumer: an eyeball, not a fixture. a frame is a 16.6 ms budget (5.9's max-lane law, where the pipeline is the gpu), and \"correct\" becomes \"measurably plausible\" — a reference image within a psnr budget, not a bitwise match. it all rests on four pieces of linear algebra, and this track's promise is that you'll never take them on faith: every formula here is executed in numpy, and you'll re-run them all."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-02.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C2 The Rasterization Pipeline","num":"Track C · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","The stages: programmable shaders between fixed-function silicon","Fragments arrive in 2×2 quads — hello, helper lanes","Barycentric interpolation: every pixel is a blend of three vertices","Perspective-correct vs affine, and z-buffer precision","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Where a frame's time actually goes","2. Overdraw: the fragment stage's silent tax","3. Reverse-Z: applied floating point (M1 L8, graphics edition)","Gotchas","Scenario — the frame that tessellation ate"],"summary":"C1's vertices become pixels here. The rasterization pipeline is a fixed sequence of stages, two of which you program (vertex and fragment) and three of which are fixed-function silicon. The whole thing runs on the same SIMT hardware you learned in Module 4 — fragments even come in 2×2 quads that create \"helper lanes,\" ","text":"c2 the rasterization pipeline the rasterization pipeline — where your shaders meet the silicon concept the stages: programmable shaders between fixed-function silicon fragments arrive in 2×2 quads — hello, helper lanes barycentric interpolation: every pixel is a blend of three vertices perspective-correct vs affine, and z-buffer precision worked example key points quiz exercises 1. where a frame's time actually goes 2. overdraw: the fragment stage's silent tax 3. reverse-z: applied floating point (m1 l8, graphics edition) gotchas scenario — the frame that tessellation ate practice c1's vertices become pixels here. the rasterization pipeline is a fixed sequence of stages, two of which you program (vertex and fragment) and three of which are fixed-function silicon. the whole thing runs on the same simt hardware you learned in module 4 — fragments even come in 2×2 quads that create \"helper lanes,\" a divergence cost hiding in plain sight. understand the pipeline as hardware and the mysterious performance cliffs of real engines become named, predictable costs."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-03.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C3 First Triangle: OpenGL to Vulkan","num":"Track C · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","The same triangle, two philosophies","Every Vulkan object is a concept you already learned","Dynamic rendering: skipping the renderpass ceremony","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The object graph, from instance to first pixel","2. Why OpenGL hit a wall (the history worth knowing)","3. What explicit ownership actually costs you","Gotchas","Scenario — the port that finally used all the cores","Practice"],"summary":"Every graphics programmer draws a first triangle. In OpenGL it's ~50 lines because the driver makes every hard decision for you; in Vulkan 1.4 it's ~1000 lines because you make them. That verbosity is not bureaucracy — it's the driver guesswork from 5.9's world (streams, sync, memory) turned into objects you own. Today","text":"c3 first triangle: opengl to vulkan first triangle — from opengl's 50 lines to vulkan's explicit machine concept the same triangle, two philosophies every vulkan object is a concept you already learned dynamic rendering: skipping the renderpass ceremony worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the object graph, from instance to first pixel 2. why opengl hit a wall (the history worth knowing) 3. what explicit ownership actually costs you gotchas scenario — the port that finally used all the cores practice every graphics programmer draws a first triangle. in opengl it's ~50 lines because the driver makes every hard decision for you; in vulkan 1.4 it's ~1000 lines because you make them. that verbosity is not bureaucracy — it's the driver guesswork from 5.9's world (streams, sync, memory) turned into objects you own. today you'll see that every intimidating vulkan object is a core concept you already learned, and that dynamic rendering (now core) cuts the worst of the ceremony."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-04.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C4 GLSL Shaders Deep Dive","num":"Track C · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","The I/O contract: in, uniform, out","Four ways to feed a shader — a latency/size table","std140/std430: the vec3 padding trap","Compilation: GLSL to SPIR-V","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The descriptor set: kernel arguments, bundled","2. The alignment rules, compactly","3. Where shaders fit the compiler story","Gotchas","Scenario — the material bug that was really a byte offset"],"summary":"Shaders are small SIMT kernels (5.2), but their power and their bugs live at the boundary — how data gets in and results get out. Today: the vertex/fragment I/O contract, the four ways to feed a shader (a latency table straight out of 4.5), and the std140 layout rules whose vec3-padding trap silently corrupts data in a","text":"c4 glsl shaders deep dive glsl shaders — the i/o contract and the alignment trap concept the i/o contract: in, uniform, out four ways to feed a shader — a latency/size table std140/std430: the vec3 padding trap compilation: glsl to spir-v worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the descriptor set: kernel arguments, bundled 2. the alignment rules, compactly 3. where shaders fit the compiler story gotchas scenario — the material bug that was really a byte offset practice shaders are small simt kernels (5.2), but their power and their bugs live at the boundary — how data gets in and results get out. today: the vertex/fragment i/o contract, the four ways to feed a shader (a latency table straight out of 4.5), and the std140 layout rules whose vec3-padding trap silently corrupts data in a way that looks like a gpu bug and is really m2 l5's strides with alignment."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-05.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C5 Textures, Sampling, Mipmaps","num":"Track C · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","Bilinear filtering: a sample is a blend of four texels","Mipmaps: prefiltered shrinks chosen by derivatives","sRGB and gamma: 0.5 is not half-bright","Compressed formats: textures are bandwidth citizens","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The filtering ladder","2. The color-space pipeline, drawn","3. Textures as the bandwidth budget","Gotchas","Scenario — the muddy game that a units error explained"],"summary":"A texture lookup looks trivial and hides three deep ideas: filtering (blending texels so surfaces don't pixelate), mipmaps (prefiltered shrinks chosen by the quad's derivatives from C2), and gamma (why 0.5 is not half-bright, and why lighting must happen in linear space). Get gamma wrong and every image in your game is","text":"c5 textures, sampling, mipmaps textures, sampling & mipmaps — filtering, gamma, and the bandwidth budget concept bilinear filtering: a sample is a blend of four texels mipmaps: prefiltered shrinks chosen by derivatives srgb and gamma: 0.5 is not half-bright compressed formats: textures are bandwidth citizens worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the filtering ladder 2. the color-space pipeline, drawn 3. textures as the bandwidth budget gotchas scenario — the muddy game that a units error explained practice a texture lookup looks trivial and hides three deep ideas: filtering (blending texels so surfaces don't pixelate), mipmaps (prefiltered shrinks chosen by the quad's derivatives from c2), and gamma (why 0.5 is not half-bright, and why lighting must happen in linear space). get gamma wrong and every image in your game is subtly muddy; each of these is a short numpy experiment, and you'll run them."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-06.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C6 Lighting: Phong to PBR","num":"Track C · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","Phong: a plausible fake, computed by hand","PBR: light bouncing off a field of microscopic mirrors","The GGX/Smith/Schlick terms, evaluated","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The three PBR terms, and what each roughness/angle does","2. The metalness workflow (why glTF is 3 parameters)","3. Why energy conservation is the whole game","Gotchas","Scenario — the material library that finally traveled","Practice"],"summary":"Lighting is where a scene becomes believable, and it has a real history: Phong's cheap, plausible highlight gave way to physically based rendering (PBR), which models light as bouncing off microscopic mirrors and — crucially — conserves energy, so materials look right under any lighting. Every term here is a real formu","text":"c6 lighting: phong to pbr lighting — from phong's fake highlight to physically based rendering concept phong: a plausible fake, computed by hand pbr: light bouncing off a field of microscopic mirrors the ggx/smith/schlick terms, evaluated worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the three pbr terms, and what each roughness/angle does 2. the metalness workflow (why gltf is 3 parameters) 3. why energy conservation is the whole game gotchas scenario — the material library that finally traveled practice lighting is where a scene becomes believable, and it has a real history: phong's cheap, plausible highlight gave way to physically based rendering (pbr), which models light as bouncing off microscopic mirrors and — crucially — conserves energy, so materials look right under any lighting. every term here is a real formula you'll evaluate in numpy, and the whole thing only works in the linear space c5 fought for."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-07.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C7 Compute Shaders","num":"Track C · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","The reunion: every core idea, in GLSL","Compute in the frame: post-processing and simulation","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Where compute shaders sit","2. The compute toolkit is the core toolkit","3. Subgroups: warps in graphics","Gotchas","Scenario — the effect that shipped because the core transferred","Practice"],"summary":"This is the lesson where graphics and everything you learned in Module 5 turn out to be the same thing. A GLSL compute shader has workgroups (blocks), shared memory, barriers, and subgroups (warps) — you can write the 5.8 reduction in it almost verbatim, and feel the déjà vu. That's the point: the core was never CUDA, ","text":"c7 compute shaders compute shaders — the reunion, where the core comes home concept the reunion: every core idea, in glsl compute in the frame: post-processing and simulation worked example key points quiz exercises 1. where compute shaders sit 2. the compute toolkit is the core toolkit 3. subgroups: warps in graphics gotchas scenario — the effect that shipped because the core transferred practice this is the lesson where graphics and everything you learned in module 5 turn out to be the same thing. a glsl compute shader has workgroups (blocks), shared memory, barriers, and subgroups (warps) — you can write the 5.8 reduction in it almost verbatim, and feel the déjà vu. that's the point: the core was never cuda, it was the gpu. today: compute shaders in the graphics pipeline, and the post-processing and simulation work they unlock."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-08.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C8 Vulkan Sync — Command Buffers & Barriers","num":"Track C · Lesson 8","heads":["Concept","Record, submit, reuse: command buffers","The sync chapter: barriers, semaphores, fences, timeline semaphores","The barrier: make WHOSE writes visible to WHOM, WHEN","Frames-in-flight: 5.9's ring, and the swapchain dance","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The four sync primitives, and when to reach for each","2. Image layouts: the graphics-only wrinkle","3. Frames-in-flight and the swapchain, step by step","Gotchas","Scenario — the flicker that only some players saw"],"summary":"Vulkan's synchronization is the chapter that breaks people — and it's the one you're most prepared for, because it's 5.9's events-and-streams model with new names. Command buffers are recorded launches, semaphores and fences are events, barriers make writes visible before reads, and frames-in-flight is 5.9's depth-3 ri","text":"c8 vulkan sync — command buffers & barriers vulkan sync — the explicit machine, and why it's just 5.9 renamed concept record, submit, reuse: command buffers the sync chapter: barriers, semaphores, fences, timeline semaphores the barrier: make whose writes visible to whom, when frames-in-flight: 5.9's ring, and the swapchain dance worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the four sync primitives, and when to reach for each 2. image layouts: the graphics-only wrinkle 3. frames-in-flight and the swapchain, step by step gotchas scenario — the flicker that only some players saw practice vulkan's synchronization is the chapter that breaks people — and it's the one you're most prepared for, because it's 5.9's events-and-streams model with new names. command buffers are recorded launches, semaphores and fences are events, barriers make writes visible before reads, and frames-in-flight is 5.9's depth-3 ring verbatim. today: the translation table that makes vulkan sync tractable, and the validation layers that are 5.4's sanitizer for it."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-09.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C9 Ray Tracing","num":"Track C · Lesson 9","heads":["Concept","The BVH: log-N culling, M1's log lesson cashing in","The RT pipeline: the function-pointer table returns","Ray-triangle intersection, executed","The denoising bargain: 1 spp is noise","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The two ways to trace: pipeline vs ray query","2. RT cores: 4.4's specialization, third time","3. Hybrid rendering and the ray budget","Gotchas","Scenario — the reflections that fit the budget"],"summary":"Ray tracing shoots rays into a scene and asks what they hit. Done naively it's O(rays × triangles) — impossible in real time. Three things make it feasible: a BVH that turns the triangle search into O(log N) (M1's log lesson finally cashing in), RT cores that do the traversal in fixed-function silicon (4.4's specializa","text":"c9 ray tracing ray tracing — log-n culling, fixed-function cores, and the denoising bargain concept the bvh: log-n culling, m1's log lesson cashing in the rt pipeline: the function-pointer table returns ray-triangle intersection, executed the denoising bargain: 1 spp is noise worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the two ways to trace: pipeline vs ray query 2. rt cores: 4.4's specialization, third time 3. hybrid rendering and the ray budget gotchas scenario — the reflections that fit the budget practice ray tracing shoots rays into a scene and asks what they hit. done naively it's o(rays × triangles) — impossible in real time. three things make it feasible: a bvh that turns the triangle search into o(log n) (m1's log lesson finally cashing in), rt cores that do the traversal in fixed-function silicon (4.4's specialization story), and denoising, because a real-time budget affords only ~1 sample per pixel, which is pure noise. today: the ray-triangle test executed in numpy, the rt pipeline as the function-pointer table 3.6 promised, and the temporal-accumulation bargain."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-10.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C10 Frame Profiling — RenderDoc & Nsight","num":"Track C · Lesson 10","heads":["Concept","Capture where, then measure why — the two-tool ritual","The frame pathologies are all core lessons","The 10-minute frame triage runbook","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The two-tier toolset (where vs why)","2. The pathology → core-lesson → fix table","3. The runbook, and why order matters","Gotchas","Scenario — the day saved by a capture","Practice"],"summary":"You never optimize a frame by guessing — that's 5.10's whole doctrine, refitted for graphics. RenderDoc captures a frame and tells you WHERE the time goes (which pass, which draw); Nsight Graphics and Radeon GPU Profiler tell you WHY (occupancy, bandwidth, stalls). The frame pathologies you'll hunt are all core lessons","text":"c10 frame profiling — renderdoc & nsight frame profiling — capture where, then measure why concept capture where, then measure why — the two-tool ritual the frame pathologies are all core lessons the 10-minute frame triage runbook worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the two-tier toolset (where vs why) 2. the pathology → core-lesson → fix table 3. the runbook, and why order matters gotchas scenario — the day saved by a capture practice you never optimize a frame by guessing — that's 5.10's whole doctrine, refitted for graphics. renderdoc captures a frame and tells you where the time goes (which pass, which draw); nsight graphics and radeon gpu profiler tell you why (occupancy, bandwidth, stalls). the frame pathologies you'll hunt are all core lessons wearing frame clothes — overdraw, tiny draws, fat g-buffers, sync bubbles. today: the two-tool ritual and the 10-minute frame-triage runbook."},{"url":"track-c/lesson-11.html","kind":"track-c","group":"Track C · Graphics","title":"C11 Capstone — A Small Renderer with a Ray-Traced Effect","num":"Track C · Lesson 11","heads":["Concept","The spec: glTF in, a shadowed PBR frame with an RT effect out","The trust stack: how you KNOW the frame is correct AND fast","The frame budget: 16.6 ms, spent per pass","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The trust stack, layer by layer","2. The per-pass budget as an engineering contract","3. The defense bank (rehearse; #7 is the closer)","Gotchas","Scenario — the capstone that got the interview to say yes","Practice"],"summary":"Everything in Track C converges into one deliverable: a Vulkan renderer that loads a glTF model, lights it with PBR, casts a shadow, adds one ray-traced effect, and — crucially — is budgeted per pass and tested against reference images. Mirroring 5.11's ship standard: a spec, a trust stack, a per-pass frame budget, and","text":"c11 capstone — a small renderer with a ray-traced effect capstone — a small renderer, budgeted and tested, with one ray-traced effect concept the spec: gltf in, a shadowed pbr frame with an rt effect out the trust stack: how you know the frame is correct and fast the frame budget: 16.6 ms, spent per pass worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the trust stack, layer by layer 2. the per-pass budget as an engineering contract 3. the defense bank (rehearse; #7 is the closer) gotchas scenario — the capstone that got the interview to say yes practice everything in track c converges into one deliverable: a vulkan renderer that loads a gltf model, lights it with pbr, casts a shadow, adds one ray-traced effect, and — crucially — is budgeted per pass and tested against reference images. mirroring 5.11's ship standard: a spec, a trust stack, a per-pass frame budget, and a defense bank. build this, put it on github, and you can walk an interviewer through a real frame you shipped."},{"url":"track-d/lab-webgpu.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"WebGPU Live Lab","num":"dlab","heads":["The kernel (WGSL) — read it against CUDA"],"summary":"This page runs a real GPU compute kernel — a parallel sum reduction — on your hardware, with no install and no server. It is the universal GPU lab from D5: the same warps/tiles/barriers ideas from the core (5.5, 5.8), expressed in WGSL, executed by the browser. Open your devtools console to watch it work; read the WGSL","text":"webgpu live lab webgpu live lab — the 5.8 reduction, on your gpu, right now the kernel (wgsl) — read it against cuda this page runs a real gpu compute kernel — a parallel sum reduction — on your hardware, with no install and no server. it is the universal gpu lab from d5: the same warps/tiles/barriers ideas from the core (5.5, 5.8), expressed in wgsl, executed by the browser. open your devtools console to watch it work; read the wgsl below to see how little it differs from cuda."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-01.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D1 The Portability Problem & the 2026 Landscape","num":"Track D · Lesson 1","heads":["Concept","Why portability stopped being optional","The translation table — the track's spine","Four families of portable code","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The abstraction ladder, rung by rung","2. The 90/10 rule, quantified","3. Reading the 2026 machine landscape as a strategy signal","Gotchas","Scenario — the supply-crunch migration nobody wanted","Practice"],"summary":"Five modules taught you the GPU in NVIDIA's dialect: warps, shared memory, coalescing, streams, reductions. This track proves those were the IDEAS, not the vendor — the same primitives run under new names on AMD, Intel, and every web browser. Today we map the whole territory: why portability stopped being optional in 2","text":"d1 the portability problem & the 2026 landscape the portability problem — your cuda skills were never about cuda concept why portability stopped being optional the translation table — the track's spine four families of portable code worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the abstraction ladder, rung by rung 2. the 90/10 rule, quantified 3. reading the 2026 machine landscape as a strategy signal gotchas scenario — the supply-crunch migration nobody wanted practice five modules taught you the gpu in nvidia's dialect: warps, shared memory, coalescing, streams, reductions. this track proves those were the ideas, not the vendor — the same primitives run under new names on amd, intel, and every web browser. today we map the whole territory: why portability stopped being optional in 2026, the one translation table that is this track's spine, and the four families of portable code you'll spend the next eight lessons inside."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-02.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D2 ROCm & HIP","num":"Track D · Lesson 2","heads":["Concept","hipify: the diff really is renames","The one row that isn't a rename: wavefront = 64","The ROCm stack: same shape, new labels","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. What hipify handles, and the 10% it can't","2. The blind-port bug taxonomy","3. MI-series memory and the redrawn roofline","Gotchas","Scenario — the accuracy regression that was one integer","Practice"],"summary":"HIP is the lowest rung of D1's ladder: AMD's answer to \"how do I run my CUDA on your GPU\" is to give you an API that is CUDA with the identifiers renamed. The port really is that easy — the diff you'll measure is mechanical — right up until the single place it isn't: NVIDIA's warp is 32 lanes and AMD's wavefront is 64.","text":"d2 rocm & hip rocm & hip — cuda with the labels swapped, and one number that isn't concept hipify: the diff really is renames the one row that isn't a rename: wavefront = 64 the rocm stack: same shape, new labels worked example key points quiz exercises 1. what hipify handles, and the 10% it can't 2. the blind-port bug taxonomy 3. mi-series memory and the redrawn roofline gotchas scenario — the accuracy regression that was one integer practice hip is the lowest rung of d1's ladder: amd's answer to \"how do i run my cuda on your gpu\" is to give you an api that is cuda with the identifiers renamed. the port really is that easy — the diff you'll measure is mechanical — right up until the single place it isn't: nvidia's warp is 32 lanes and amd's wavefront is 64. this lesson is that one true difference, executed until it draws blood, wrapped in the stack that makes everything else feel like home."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-03.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D3 SYCL & oneAPI","num":"Track D · Lesson 3","heads":["Concept","Single-source: host and device in one file","The runtime infers the graph you used to declare","nd_range is grid/block, and reductions come in the box","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Buffers/accessors vs USM: the model decision","2. Queues, ordering, and host tasks","3. SYCL compiles through SPIR-V — the connection to D4 and D7","Gotchas","Scenario — the pipeline that \"SYCL made slow\"","Practice"],"summary":"HIP kept you writing C++-per-vendor. SYCL climbs a rung: one standard C++ source, compiled to any backend — NVIDIA, AMD, Intel, or the CPU in this sandbox. The trade is that you stop declaring the plumbing (5.3's copies, 5.9's streams) and start declaring your intent — which data each kernel reads and writes — and the ","text":"d3 sycl & oneapi sycl & oneapi — one c++ file, and a runtime that schedules for you concept single-source: host and device in one file the runtime infers the graph you used to declare nd_range is grid/block, and reductions come in the box worked example key points quiz exercises 1. buffers/accessors vs usm: the model decision 2. queues, ordering, and host tasks 3. sycl compiles through spir-v — the connection to d4 and d7 gotchas scenario — the pipeline that \"sycl made slow\" practice hip kept you writing c++-per-vendor. sycl climbs a rung: one standard c++ source, compiled to any backend — nvidia, amd, intel, or the cpu in this sandbox. the trade is that you stop declaring the plumbing (5.3's copies, 5.9's streams) and start declaring your intent — which data each kernel reads and writes — and the runtime infers the copies and the schedule for you. today you'll see that inference is not magic: it's the same dependency dag you built by hand in 5.9, discovered from your accessors."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-04.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D4 OpenCL Today","num":"Track D · Lesson 4","heads":["Concept","The boilerplate, once — and the kernel is a string","Why OpenCL lost: ecosystem, not ergonomics","Where it still wins, and SPIR-V's second life","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The setup ceremony, annotated","2. cl_mem vs SVM, and the shape of memory","3. Why the history lesson matters for your next platform bet","Gotchas","Scenario — the two teams and the tool-shaped hole","Practice"],"summary":"OpenCL was the first serious \"write once, run on any accelerator\" standard — years before HIP, SYCL, or WebGPU. It lost the desktop and datacenter to CUDA anyway, and the reason is one of the most useful engineering-history lessons you'll get: the better ecosystem beats the better spec, every time. But \"lost the war\" i","text":"d4 opencl today opencl today — the standard that lost the war and won the long tail concept the boilerplate, once — and the kernel is a string why opencl lost: ecosystem, not ergonomics where it still wins, and spir-v's second life worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the setup ceremony, annotated 2. cl_mem vs svm, and the shape of memory 3. why the history lesson matters for your next platform bet gotchas scenario — the two teams and the tool-shaped hole practice opencl was the first serious \"write once, run on any accelerator\" standard — years before hip, sycl, or webgpu. it lost the desktop and datacenter to cuda anyway, and the reason is one of the most useful engineering-history lessons you'll get: the better ecosystem beats the better spec, every time. but \"lost the war\" isn't \"died\" — opencl runs the embedded and fpga long tail, and via spir-v it quietly became the plumbing under sycl. today: the boilerplate you should see exactly once, why it lost, and where it's still the only answer."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-05.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D5 WebGPU & WGSL","num":"Track D · Lesson 5","heads":["Concept","The universal lab: adapter to device to queue to pipeline","WGSL: a CUDA kernel with decorators","The baseline versus extensions honesty","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. Buffers, bind groups, and the pipeline","2. Baseline vs extensions, and honest timing","3. The same WGSL, outside the browser","Gotchas","Scenario — the demo that only worked on the presenter's laptop","Practice"],"summary":"Every other backend in this track needs a driver, a toolchain, or specific hardware. WebGPU needs a browser — which means ~5 billion devices are now GPU-compute targets you reach with zero install. Today you will see that a WGSL compute shader is a CUDA kernel with decorators, run the 5.8 reduction on your own GPU from","text":"d5 webgpu & wgsl webgpu & wgsl — the universal gpu lab you already have installed concept the universal lab: adapter to device to queue to pipeline wgsl: a cuda kernel with decorators the baseline versus extensions honesty worked example key points quiz exercises 1. buffers, bind groups, and the pipeline 2. baseline vs extensions, and honest timing 3. the same wgsl, outside the browser gotchas scenario — the demo that only worked on the presenter's laptop practice every other backend in this track needs a driver, a toolchain, or specific hardware. webgpu needs a browser — which means ~5 billion devices are now gpu-compute targets you reach with zero install. today you will see that a wgsl compute shader is a cuda kernel with decorators, run the 5.8 reduction on your own gpu from a web page, and learn the one discipline the browser forces on you: code to the guaranteed baseline, feature-detect everything else."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-06.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D6 Kokkos & RAJA","num":"Track D · Lesson 6","heads":["Concept","View: strides with a compile-time layout switch","Spaces as types: where code runs, where data lives","One parallel_for, every backend — and it runs for real on the CPU","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. View: layout, memory space, and traits","2. Spaces, deep_copy, and the team hierarchy","3. Kokkos vs RAJA vs std::execution, and why labs bet on it","Gotchas","Scenario — the code that had to outlive three supercomputers","Practice"],"summary":"SYCL gave you one source and an inferred schedule. Kokkos goes further: it makes the two decisions that actually determine GPU performance — memory layout (M2 L7's AoS/SoA) and where code runs (5.3's two worlds) — into compile-time template parameters , so one source file is coalesced on the GPU and cache-friendly on t","text":"d6 kokkos & raja kokkos & raja — the performance-portability bet the national labs made concept view: strides with a compile-time layout switch spaces as types: where code runs, where data lives one parallel_for, every backend — and it runs for real on the cpu worked example key points quiz exercises 1. view: layout, memory space, and traits 2. spaces, deep_copy, and the team hierarchy 3. kokkos vs raja vs std::execution, and why labs bet on it gotchas scenario — the code that had to outlive three supercomputers practice sycl gave you one source and an inferred schedule. kokkos goes further: it makes the two decisions that actually determine gpu performance — memory layout (m2 l7's aos/soa) and where code runs (5.3's two worlds) — into compile-time template parameters , so one source file is coalesced on the gpu and cache-friendly on the cpu without you editing a line. this is why llnl runs it on el capitan and ~300 hpc projects bet decades of code on it. today: views, spaces, and the single parallel_for that compiles to every machine."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-07.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D7 Triton & MLIR","num":"Track D · Lesson 7","heads":["Concept","Portable by IR: one Python kernel, two vendor ISAs","Why compilers are eating this problem: the dial turned by machines","The honest limit: vendor libraries still own the peak","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The lowering pipeline, stage by stage","2. Autotuning: 5.6's dial, searched by machine","3. Where compilers win, and where they don't (yet)","Gotchas","Scenario — the porting team that automated itself out of a job (the good way)","Practice"],"summary":"Every rung so far asked you to port: rename to HIP, rewrite into SYCL accessors, feature-detect in WGSL. The compiler rung inverts it. You write one kernel in Triton (the language from Track A's A4), and the compiler lowers it through MLIR to NVIDIA's PTX or AMD's AMDGCN — same source, different ISA, zero edits. And be","text":"d7 triton & mlir triton & mlir — let the compiler do the porting concept portable by ir: one python kernel, two vendor isas why compilers are eating this problem: the dial turned by machines the honest limit: vendor libraries still own the peak worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the lowering pipeline, stage by stage 2. autotuning: 5.6's dial, searched by machine 3. where compilers win, and where they don't (yet) gotchas scenario — the porting team that automated itself out of a job (the good way) practice every rung so far asked you to port: rename to hip, rewrite into sycl accessors, feature-detect in wgsl. the compiler rung inverts it. you write one kernel in triton (the language from track a's a4), and the compiler lowers it through mlir to nvidia's ptx or amd's amdgcn — same source, different isa, zero edits. and because the compiler re-tunes per architecture, it does the one thing a human porter can't do at scale: turn 5.6's occupancy dial optimally for every backend, automatically. today: how the lowering works, why compilers are eating this problem, and the honest limit where they still lose to vendor libraries."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-08.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D8 Honest Cross-Vendor Benchmarking","num":"Track D · Lesson 8","heads":["Concept","Wall time is the wrong denominator","The only fair speed number: % of your own roofline","The five sins, and the fairness protocol","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The normalization formulas","2. The fair-comparison protocol (invert the five sins)","3. The benchmark README standard (5.11's stranger test, multi-vendor)","Gotchas","Scenario — the procurement that turned on a denominator","Practice"],"summary":"Now that you can run one algorithm on four vendors, you'll be asked the loaded question: \"which GPU is faster?\" It's a trap, because wall-time-faster is almost never the decision — a premium card can win the demo and lose the datacenter on cost and watts. This lesson is the measurement discipline that makes cross-vendo","text":"d8 honest cross-vendor benchmarking honest cross-vendor benchmarking — the number that survives a rewrite concept wall time is the wrong denominator the only fair speed number: % of your own roofline the five sins, and the fairness protocol worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the normalization formulas 2. the fair-comparison protocol (invert the five sins) 3. the benchmark readme standard (5.11's stranger test, multi-vendor) gotchas scenario — the procurement that turned on a denominator practice now that you can run one algorithm on four vendors, you'll be asked the loaded question: \"which gpu is faster?\" it's a trap, because wall-time-faster is almost never the decision — a premium card can win the demo and lose the datacenter on cost and watts. this lesson is the measurement discipline that makes cross-vendor comparisons honest: normalize by dollars and joules (4.6, completed), compare each machine to its own roofline (4.7), and ship a benchmark a stranger can't dismiss (5.11). plus the five sins to spot in any vendor's slide."},{"url":"track-d/lesson-09.html","kind":"track-d","group":"Track D · Portable","title":"D9 Capstone — One Algorithm, Four Backends","num":"Track D · Lesson 9","heads":["Concept","The spec: one algorithm, four backends, one fixture","The trust stack: how you KNOW each port is right","The portability report: the actual deliverable","Worked example","Key points","Quiz","Exercises","1. The trust stack, per backend","2. The filled concept-translation table (the report's centerpiece)","3. The defense bank (rehearse these; the last one is the closer)","Gotchas","Scenario — the report that got the offer","Practice"],"summary":"The whole track reduces to one claim: the core taught ideas, not a vendor. This capstone proves it by running a single algorithm — the 5.8 reduction — on four backends (CUDA, HIP, SYCL or Kokkos, WebGPU), judging all four with one shared fixture set, and writing the portability report that is the actual portfolio piece","text":"d9 capstone — one algorithm, four backends capstone — one reduction, four backends, one honest report concept the spec: one algorithm, four backends, one fixture the trust stack: how you know each port is right the portability report: the actual deliverable worked example key points quiz exercises 1. the trust stack, per backend 2. the filled concept-translation table (the report's centerpiece) 3. the defense bank (rehearse these; the last one is the closer) gotchas scenario — the report that got the offer practice the whole track reduces to one claim: the core taught ideas, not a vendor. this capstone proves it by running a single algorithm — the 5.8 reduction — on four backends (cuda, hip, sycl or kokkos, webgpu), judging all four with one shared fixture set, and writing the portability report that is the actual portfolio piece. mirroring 5.11's ship standard: a spec, a trust stack, the rungs, and a defense bank. build this, put it on github, and you can defend cross-vendor gpu work in any interview."}];
